1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,750
Fire electrons, one at a time, at a barrier with two narrow openings, and

2
00:00:04,750 --> 00:00:07,888
record where each one lands on a screen behind it.

3
00:00:07,888 --> 00:00:13,288
Each electron arrives as a single, sharp dot, at a place nobody can predict.

4
00:00:13,288 --> 00:00:15,812
Ten electrons look like random noise.

5
00:00:15,812 --> 00:00:18,204
A hundred, still noise.

6
00:00:18,204 --> 00:00:22,029
But by three thousand, a pattern is appearing, and by seventy thousand it's

7
00:00:22,029 --> 00:00:23,229
unmistakable:

8
00:00:23,229 --> 00:00:24,267
stripes.

9
00:00:24,267 --> 00:00:27,854
Bright bands where many electrons land, dark bands where almost none ever

10
00:00:27,854 --> 00:00:28,642
do.

11
00:00:28,642 --> 00:00:32,004
That's an interference pattern, the signature of a wave.

12
00:00:32,004 --> 00:00:37,217
Akira Tonomura's team at Hitachi recorded this buildup in 1989, one electron

13
00:00:37,217 --> 00:00:42,179
at a time, at these same five counts, using an electron biprism, the electron

14
00:00:42,179 --> 00:00:45,179
version of two slits.

15
00:00:45,338 --> 00:00:47,125
So what goes through the slits?

16
00:00:47,125 --> 00:00:50,838
Here's the answer quantum mechanics gives, computed by solving its central

17
00:00:50,838 --> 00:00:52,475
equation numerically.

18
00:00:52,475 --> 00:00:56,825
Before detection, the electron is described by a wave, a complex number at

19
00:00:56,825 --> 00:00:58,363
every point in space;

20
00:00:58,363 --> 00:01:01,700
the color shows its phase, the brightness its size.

21
00:01:01,700 --> 00:01:05,388
The wave reaches the barrier, and part of it passes through both openings

22
00:01:05,388 --> 00:01:06,363
at once.

23
00:01:06,363 --> 00:01:11,021
The two halves spread out and overlap, and where they meet, they interfere.

24
00:01:11,021 --> 00:01:15,096
When the wave reaches the screen, the electron is found at a single point,

25
00:01:15,096 --> 00:01:19,408
at random, and the probability of each point is the size of the wave there,

26
00:01:19,408 --> 00:01:20,496
squared.

27
00:01:20,496 --> 00:01:23,683
Do it seventy thousand times and you get the stripes.

28
00:01:23,683 --> 00:01:25,908
The wave is never seen directly;

29
00:01:25,908 --> 00:01:29,046
it's what decides the odds.

30
00:01:29,187 --> 00:01:31,500
The wave obeys one equation.

31
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:36,475
Erwin Schrödinger published it in a series of papers in 1926, a hundred years

32
00:01:36,475 --> 00:01:41,262
ago this year, and that same year Max Born proposed what the wave means:

33
00:01:41,262 --> 00:01:45,825
the probability of finding the particle is the size of psi, squared.

34
00:01:45,825 --> 00:01:50,387
Everything you just watched follows from these two ideas.

35
00:01:50,912 --> 00:01:54,700
In this video we'll build quantum mechanics from the ground up, with the mathematics

36
00:01:54,700 --> 00:01:56,687
on screen at every step.

37
00:01:56,687 --> 00:01:58,137
First, amplitudes:

38
00:01:58,137 --> 00:02:01,375
why probabilities don't add, but complex arrows do.

39
00:02:01,375 --> 00:02:03,337
Then the Schrödinger equation itself:

40
00:02:03,337 --> 00:02:06,925
where it comes from, and what it does to wave packets, particles in boxes,

41
00:02:06,925 --> 00:02:08,200
and barriers.

42
00:02:08,200 --> 00:02:10,075
Then the mathematical framework:

43
00:02:10,075 --> 00:02:14,450
states as vectors, observables as operators, the commutator of position and

44
00:02:14,450 --> 00:02:17,962
momentum, and the uncertainty principle, derived.

45
00:02:17,962 --> 00:02:20,512
Then two worlds we can solve exactly:

46
00:02:20,512 --> 00:02:23,612
the harmonic oscillator and the hydrogen atom.

47
00:02:23,612 --> 00:02:27,825
And finally spin and entanglement, ending with Bell's theorem.

48
00:02:27,825 --> 00:02:32,437
All you need is complex numbers, calculus and a little linear algebra.

49
00:02:38,131 --> 00:02:42,431
Let's go back to the double slit, and do it more carefully, using the simulation

50
00:02:42,431 --> 00:02:44,056
from the start of the video.

51
00:02:44,056 --> 00:02:46,319
First, close the bottom slit.

52
00:02:46,319 --> 00:02:50,269
Electrons arriving at the screen pile up in one broad hump.

53
00:02:50,269 --> 00:02:53,073
Call that distribution P one.

54
00:02:53,073 --> 00:02:56,486
Now close the top slit instead, and open the bottom one:

55
00:02:56,486 --> 00:02:58,536
another hump, P two.

56
00:02:58,536 --> 00:03:02,861
Common sense says that with both slits open, each electron goes through one

57
00:03:02,861 --> 00:03:07,244
slit or the other, so the two distributions should simply add.

58
00:03:07,244 --> 00:03:08,719
That's not what happens.

59
00:03:08,719 --> 00:03:11,869
With both slits open, the distribution has stripes.

60
00:03:11,869 --> 00:03:13,506
And look at the dark places:

61
00:03:13,506 --> 00:03:17,081
there, with either slit alone, plenty of electrons arrive.

62
00:03:17,081 --> 00:03:19,419
Open both, and almost none do.

63
00:03:19,419 --> 00:03:23,106
Adding a second way to get somewhere can make it impossible to get there.

64
00:03:23,106 --> 00:03:26,219
Probabilities don't add.

65
00:03:26,711 --> 00:03:28,773
Here's the rule that does work.

66
00:03:28,773 --> 00:03:32,211
Each way of getting from the source to a point on the screen contributes an

67
00:03:32,211 --> 00:03:33,098
arrow:

68
00:03:33,098 --> 00:03:35,736
a complex number called an amplitude.

69
00:03:35,736 --> 00:03:39,873
Add the arrows tip to tail, and the probability is the length of the total,

70
00:03:39,873 --> 00:03:41,377
squared.

71
00:03:41,377 --> 00:03:44,077
Now change the angle between the two arrows.

72
00:03:44,077 --> 00:03:48,352
When they point the same way, the total is twice as long, and the probability

73
00:03:48,352 --> 00:03:51,052
is four times what one slit gives.

74
00:03:51,052 --> 00:03:55,077
When they point in opposite directions, they cancel, and the probability is

75
00:03:55,077 --> 00:03:56,140
zero.

76
00:03:56,140 --> 00:04:00,002
On average you get the sum of the two separate probabilities, but at any one

77
00:04:00,002 --> 00:04:04,702
point, you can get anything from zero to double that.

78
00:04:05,152 --> 00:04:06,852
Let's see exactly why.

79
00:04:06,852 --> 00:04:11,527
The length squared of a complex number is the number times its complex conjugate.

80
00:04:11,527 --> 00:04:12,727
Multiply out:

81
00:04:12,727 --> 00:04:16,427
you get the two separate probabilities, plus two cross terms.

82
00:04:16,427 --> 00:04:20,002
The cross terms are conjugates of each other, so together they're twice the

83
00:04:20,002 --> 00:04:21,227
real part.

84
00:04:21,227 --> 00:04:25,827
Write each arrow as a length times e to the i phi, and the cross term becomes

85
00:04:25,827 --> 00:04:28,519
a cosine of the angle between them.

86
00:04:28,519 --> 00:04:31,981
The first two terms are what you'd get if probabilities added.

87
00:04:31,981 --> 00:04:33,519
The last one is new.

88
00:04:33,519 --> 00:04:37,469
It's called the interference term, and because a cosine can be negative, it

89
00:04:37,469 --> 00:04:39,531
can take probability away.

90
00:04:39,531 --> 00:04:41,056
That's the whole difference:

91
00:04:41,056 --> 00:04:46,344
in quantum mechanics, amplitudes add, and you square at the end.

92
00:04:46,944 --> 00:04:48,681
So where do the angles come from?

93
00:04:48,681 --> 00:04:50,131
Feynman's picture:

94
00:04:50,131 --> 00:04:54,269
each amplitude is like the hand of a tiny clock that the electron carries

95
00:04:54,269 --> 00:04:55,681
along its path.

96
00:04:55,681 --> 00:04:59,894
The hand turns one full revolution for every wavelength traveled.

97
00:04:59,894 --> 00:05:03,581
Two paths of different lengths arrive with their hands turned by different

98
00:05:03,581 --> 00:05:05,019
amounts.

99
00:05:05,019 --> 00:05:05,969
In symbols:

100
00:05:05,969 --> 00:05:09,656
a path of length r turns the hand by two pi r over lambda.

101
00:05:09,656 --> 00:05:13,269
So the angle between the two arrows is two pi times the difference in path

102
00:05:13,269 --> 00:05:15,044
lengths, in wavelengths.

103
00:05:15,044 --> 00:05:18,681
When the screen is far away, that difference is d times y over L.

104
00:05:18,681 --> 00:05:22,344
The arrows line up, and the screen is bright, whenever the difference is a

105
00:05:22,344 --> 00:05:24,081
whole number of wavelengths:

106
00:05:24,081 --> 00:05:26,181
at heights m lambda L over d.

107
00:05:26,181 --> 00:05:28,706
And the wavelength itself comes from de Broglie:

108
00:05:28,706 --> 00:05:31,419
lambda equals h over the momentum.

109
00:05:31,419 --> 00:05:37,969
For electrons accelerated through fifty kilovolts, that's about five picometers.

110
00:05:38,552 --> 00:05:40,927
Let's check that against the simulation.

111
00:05:40,927 --> 00:05:46,977
Its wavelength is 8.38 units, the slits are 60 apart, and the screen is 512

112
00:05:46,977 --> 00:05:47,890
away.

113
00:05:47,890 --> 00:05:51,502
So the fringes should be 71.5 units apart.

114
00:05:51,502 --> 00:05:53,565
Measured from the simulated pattern:

115
00:05:53,565 --> 00:05:54,777
71.0.

116
00:05:54,777 --> 00:05:58,802
And the dark fringes sit exactly where the two paths differ by a half-integer

117
00:05:58,802 --> 00:06:00,711
number of wavelengths.

118
00:06:00,711 --> 00:06:03,586
Two slits, two paths, two arrows.

119
00:06:03,586 --> 00:06:06,523
But an electron isn't limited to two options.

120
00:06:06,523 --> 00:06:11,111
In general there's an amplitude for the electron to be at every point in space,

121
00:06:11,111 --> 00:06:12,736
and that's what we turn to next.

122
00:06:14,161 --> 00:06:18,774
In the double slit, each path contributed one arrow, one complex number.

123
00:06:18,774 --> 00:06:21,599
Now let an electron be anywhere on a line.

124
00:06:21,599 --> 00:06:25,049
Quantum mechanics assigns it an arrow at every point x:

125
00:06:25,049 --> 00:06:29,486
a complex number whose length and direction can change from place to place.

126
00:06:29,486 --> 00:06:35,332
That assignment, x goes to a complex number, is the wavefunction, psi of x.

127
00:06:35,332 --> 00:06:39,182
If you have seen a quantum state drawn as an arrow with a few components,

128
00:06:39,182 --> 00:06:43,857
this is the same idea, with one component for every point on the line.

129
00:06:43,857 --> 00:06:45,770
Two things vary along x:

130
00:06:45,770 --> 00:06:50,257
how long each arrow is, and which way it points.

131
00:06:51,349 --> 00:06:56,599
To see every arrow at once, stand each one up in its own complex plane, perpendicular

132
00:06:56,599 --> 00:06:59,436
to the x axis, and connect their tips.

133
00:06:59,436 --> 00:07:02,536
The wavefunction becomes a curve in three dimensions:

134
00:07:02,536 --> 00:07:06,032
here, a helix that swells and fades.

135
00:07:06,032 --> 00:07:07,695
Now look at it from the front.

136
00:07:07,695 --> 00:07:09,582
All you see is the real part:

137
00:07:09,582 --> 00:07:12,507
the wiggly curve you find in most textbooks.

138
00:07:12,507 --> 00:07:14,245
But that's only a shadow.

139
00:07:14,245 --> 00:07:18,782
Turn it a quarter turn and you see the imaginary part, the same wiggle shifted

140
00:07:18,782 --> 00:07:20,607
by a quarter of a cycle.

141
00:07:20,607 --> 00:07:23,257
Neither is more real than the other.

142
00:07:23,257 --> 00:07:28,182
And looking straight down the x axis, every arrow lands in one complex plane:

143
00:07:28,182 --> 00:07:30,907
different lengths, pointing every which way.

144
00:07:30,907 --> 00:07:32,445
Both views matter.

145
00:07:32,445 --> 00:07:37,282
To keep both in a flat picture, we need one more idea.

146
00:07:37,716 --> 00:07:38,866
Here's the idea:

147
00:07:38,866 --> 00:07:41,728
replace the direction of each arrow with a color.

148
00:07:41,728 --> 00:07:46,466
Pointing right is red, straight up is yellow-green, left is cyan, down is

149
00:07:46,466 --> 00:07:50,778
violet, and everything in between blends smoothly around the wheel.

150
00:07:50,778 --> 00:07:57,016
The angle of a complex number is called its phase, so color will mean phase.

151
00:07:57,191 --> 00:08:00,103
So here is the same wavefunction, flattened.

152
00:08:00,103 --> 00:08:04,203
The height is the length of each arrow, and the color is its direction.

153
00:08:04,203 --> 00:08:08,053
The bands of color cycling along x are the helix's turns.

154
00:08:08,053 --> 00:08:12,203
One more change, and we'll keep this convention for the rest of the video:

155
00:08:12,203 --> 00:08:16,016
from now on, the height shows the length squared, for a reason we're about

156
00:08:16,016 --> 00:08:17,357
to see.

157
00:08:17,357 --> 00:08:18,995
What does the wavefunction mean?

158
00:08:18,995 --> 00:08:21,982
Max Born's answer, from 1926:

159
00:08:21,982 --> 00:08:25,970
the probability of finding the electron between a and b is the integral of

160
00:08:25,970 --> 00:08:29,995
the length squared, the absolute value of psi, squared.

161
00:08:29,995 --> 00:08:34,707
For this wavefunction and this interval, that's 0.556.

162
00:08:34,707 --> 00:08:39,174
And since the electron is somewhere, the total area must be one.

163
00:08:39,174 --> 00:08:42,249
So the wavefunction doesn't say where the electron is.

164
00:08:42,249 --> 00:08:44,349
It says where it might be found.

165
00:08:44,349 --> 00:08:48,661
If we prepared this same state over and over and looked, the places we'd find

166
00:08:48,661 --> 00:08:52,574
it would pile up where the height is large, and never where it's zero.

167
00:08:52,574 --> 00:08:55,261
These dots aren't a path the electron follows;

168
00:08:55,261 --> 00:08:59,174
each is just one possible outcome.

169
00:08:59,432 --> 00:09:01,820
Notice what this rule throws away.

170
00:09:01,820 --> 00:09:06,795
Multiply the whole wavefunction by the same phase factor, e to the i alpha.

171
00:09:06,795 --> 00:09:11,482
Every arrow turns by the same angle, so every color shifts together, but every

172
00:09:11,482 --> 00:09:15,682
length stays the same, so every probability stays the same.

173
00:09:15,682 --> 00:09:19,132
No experiment can ever detect this overall phase.

174
00:09:19,132 --> 00:09:22,107
It's pure bookkeeping.

175
00:09:22,253 --> 00:09:26,391
But the phase is far from meaningless when it changes from place to place.

176
00:09:26,391 --> 00:09:30,678
Here are three wavefunctions with exactly the same probability density.

177
00:09:30,678 --> 00:09:33,241
The only difference is how the colors cycle:

178
00:09:33,241 --> 00:09:37,416
one winds forward, one doesn't wind at all, one winds backward.

179
00:09:37,416 --> 00:09:42,228
Let's let them evolve, by the equation we're about to build.

180
00:09:43,903 --> 00:09:45,278
They fly apart.

181
00:09:45,278 --> 00:09:50,341
The first moves right, the second stays put and spreads, the third moves left.

182
00:09:50,341 --> 00:09:54,291
The rate at which the phase winds, radians per unit length, is called the

183
00:09:54,291 --> 00:09:59,303
wave number, k, and de Broglie's rule says the momentum is h-bar times k.

184
00:09:59,303 --> 00:10:02,778
Momentum is written in the phase.

185
00:10:03,207 --> 00:10:06,045
The purest case is a wave with a single k:

186
00:10:06,045 --> 00:10:08,595
a perfect helix, e to the i k x.

187
00:10:08,595 --> 00:10:13,495
Its density is the same everywhere, and its momentum is exactly h-bar k.

188
00:10:13,495 --> 00:10:16,795
But that means the electron could be anywhere at all.

189
00:10:16,795 --> 00:10:18,870
Sharp momentum, no position.

190
00:10:18,870 --> 00:10:20,545
Keep that tension in mind;

191
00:10:20,545 --> 00:10:23,282
it's coming back as the uncertainty principle.

192
00:10:23,282 --> 00:10:24,220
First, though:

193
00:10:24,220 --> 00:10:25,757
how do these arrows move?

194
00:10:31,224 --> 00:10:34,611
So a quantum state is a complex number at every point.

195
00:10:34,611 --> 00:10:36,199
How does it change in time?

196
00:10:36,199 --> 00:10:38,999
Whatever the rule is, it has to do three things.

197
00:10:38,999 --> 00:10:40,211
It must be linear:

198
00:10:40,211 --> 00:10:44,624
if two wavefunctions are allowed, so is their sum, because that's where interference

199
00:10:44,624 --> 00:10:45,724
comes from.

200
00:10:45,724 --> 00:10:50,174
It should be first order in time, so the wavefunction now determines the wavefunction

201
00:10:50,174 --> 00:10:51,149
later.

202
00:10:51,149 --> 00:10:56,374
And it must keep the total probability equal to one, forever.

203
00:10:56,832 --> 00:11:00,482
Here's the most direct route, the one most textbooks take.

204
00:11:00,482 --> 00:11:04,920
Start with the simplest wavefunction of all, a plane wave, e to the i k x

205
00:11:04,920 --> 00:11:06,520
minus omega t:

206
00:11:06,520 --> 00:11:09,332
a helix that spins as time goes on.

207
00:11:09,332 --> 00:11:11,795
We know two facts about it from experiment:

208
00:11:11,795 --> 00:11:16,607
its energy is h-bar omega, the Planck-Einstein relation, and its momentum

209
00:11:16,607 --> 00:11:19,315
is h-bar k, de Broglie's.

210
00:11:19,315 --> 00:11:20,728
Now differentiate.

211
00:11:20,728 --> 00:11:25,415
A time derivative brings down a factor of minus i omega, so i h-bar times

212
00:11:25,415 --> 00:11:28,115
d psi d t is h-bar omega psi:

213
00:11:28,115 --> 00:11:29,978
the energy times psi.

214
00:11:29,978 --> 00:11:35,540
A space derivative brings down i k, so minus i h-bar d psi d x is the momentum

215
00:11:35,540 --> 00:11:36,815
times psi.

216
00:11:36,815 --> 00:11:41,665
Do it twice, and minus h-bar squared times the second derivative is the momentum

217
00:11:41,665 --> 00:11:43,574
squared times psi.

218
00:11:43,574 --> 00:11:46,961
For a particle, energy is kinetic plus potential:

219
00:11:46,961 --> 00:11:49,699
p squared over two m, plus V of x.

220
00:11:49,699 --> 00:11:54,636
Replace each side by what it does to psi, and out comes the Schrödinger equation:

221
00:11:54,636 --> 00:12:00,874
i h-bar d psi d t equals minus h-bar squared over two m times the second derivative,

222
00:12:00,874 --> 00:12:02,615
plus V psi.

223
00:12:02,615 --> 00:12:04,028
Notice what happened:

224
00:12:04,028 --> 00:12:09,353
momentum became an operation, minus i h-bar d by d x, and the energy became

225
00:12:09,353 --> 00:12:11,853
an operator called the Hamiltonian, H.

226
00:12:11,853 --> 00:12:16,328
The equation is just i h-bar d psi d t equals H psi.

227
00:12:16,328 --> 00:12:17,703
This isn't a proof.

228
00:12:17,703 --> 00:12:19,378
Plane waves made the guess;

229
00:12:19,378 --> 00:12:23,040
linearity extends it to every superposition of plane waves;

230
00:12:23,040 --> 00:12:27,015
and a century of experiments confirms it.

231
00:12:27,565 --> 00:12:28,890
Why does i appear at all?

232
00:12:28,890 --> 00:12:30,578
Couldn't psi just be a real wave?

233
00:12:30,578 --> 00:12:31,603
Try it.

234
00:12:31,603 --> 00:12:34,753
Add a real wave moving right to one moving left.

235
00:12:34,753 --> 00:12:39,803
The sum is a standing wave, two sine k x cosine omega t, and a quarter of

236
00:12:39,803 --> 00:12:43,790
a period later it is zero everywhere, at the same instant.

237
00:12:43,790 --> 00:12:47,882
If psi squared were a probability, the particle would have vanished.

238
00:12:47,882 --> 00:12:50,407
Now do the same with complex waves.

239
00:12:50,407 --> 00:12:54,595
The sum is two cosine k x times e to the minus i omega t.

240
00:12:54,595 --> 00:12:59,145
The overall factor only turns every arrow together, so the probability, four

241
00:12:59,145 --> 00:13:03,720
cosine squared k x, never changes and never vanishes.

242
00:13:03,720 --> 00:13:04,957
The colors cycle;

243
00:13:04,957 --> 00:13:08,107
the density stands still.

244
00:13:08,774 --> 00:13:11,236
Here's another way to see what the i does.

245
00:13:11,236 --> 00:13:15,699
Strip the Schrödinger equation down to a free particle, in units where h-bar

246
00:13:15,699 --> 00:13:17,061
and m are one.

247
00:13:17,061 --> 00:13:21,011
Without the i, it would be the heat equation, the rule for how temperature

248
00:13:21,011 --> 00:13:22,199
smooths out.

249
00:13:22,199 --> 00:13:25,365
Start both from the same bump and let them run.

250
00:13:25,365 --> 00:13:26,340
Both spread.

251
00:13:26,340 --> 00:13:28,265
But heat flattens and fades:

252
00:13:28,265 --> 00:13:31,953
the bump loses half its size, and you can never run it backward to recover

253
00:13:31,953 --> 00:13:33,253
the original.

254
00:13:33,253 --> 00:13:37,453
The Schrödinger version spreads too, but its size, the total probability,

255
00:13:37,453 --> 00:13:40,103
stays at exactly one hundred percent.

256
00:13:40,103 --> 00:13:44,153
Instead of fading, the shape is stored in the phases, the color pattern, and

257
00:13:44,153 --> 00:13:46,628
the evolution can always be undone.

258
00:13:46,628 --> 00:13:50,615
The i turns decay into rotation.

259
00:13:50,790 --> 00:13:54,940
Rearranged, the equation says the rate of change of psi is minus i over h-bar

260
00:13:54,940 --> 00:13:56,303
times H psi.

261
00:13:56,303 --> 00:13:57,940
Read that geometrically.

262
00:13:57,940 --> 00:14:01,490
For a plane wave, H psi is just the energy times psi:

263
00:14:01,490 --> 00:14:03,678
an arrow pointing the same way.

264
00:14:03,678 --> 00:14:07,278
Multiplying by minus i turns it a quarter turn clockwise.

265
00:14:07,278 --> 00:14:11,290
So the velocity of the arrow is always perpendicular to the arrow itself,

266
00:14:11,290 --> 00:14:15,078
and an arrow whose velocity is always perpendicular to it just goes around

267
00:14:15,078 --> 00:14:21,065
in a circle, at angular speed E over h-bar, never changing length.

268
00:14:21,065 --> 00:14:26,140
For a general wavefunction, H psi isn't parallel to psi, because the second-derivative

269
00:14:26,140 --> 00:14:28,753
term depends on how the wave curves.

270
00:14:28,753 --> 00:14:32,953
Each arrow is pushed sideways by its neighbors, and that's what makes waves

271
00:14:32,953 --> 00:14:35,490
travel, spread and interfere.

272
00:14:35,490 --> 00:14:39,753
But the quarter turn is always there, and it's what will keep the total probability

273
00:14:39,753 --> 00:14:41,915
fixed.

274
00:14:42,065 --> 00:14:43,278
Let's prove that.

275
00:14:43,278 --> 00:14:46,790
How fast does the probability density change at one point?

276
00:14:46,790 --> 00:14:52,190
By the product rule, it's psi star times d psi d t, plus psi times d psi star

277
00:14:52,190 --> 00:14:52,953
d t.

278
00:14:52,953 --> 00:14:56,378
Use the Schrödinger equation for each time derivative.

279
00:14:56,378 --> 00:15:00,815
The potential terms cancel each other, as long as V is real, and what's left

280
00:15:00,815 --> 00:15:04,074
is a perfect derivative with respect to x.

281
00:15:04,074 --> 00:15:08,324
That's a continuity equation, the same law that says fluid can't appear or

282
00:15:08,324 --> 00:15:10,511
disappear, only flow.

283
00:15:10,511 --> 00:15:15,524
The quantity flowing is the probability current, j, h-bar over m times the

284
00:15:15,524 --> 00:15:18,486
imaginary part of psi star psi prime.

285
00:15:18,486 --> 00:15:20,586
Probability only moves around;

286
00:15:20,586 --> 00:15:23,432
it is never created or destroyed.

287
00:15:23,432 --> 00:15:26,845
Here's a moving packet with its current drawn underneath.

288
00:15:26,845 --> 00:15:30,607
The current pushes probability forward at the front, it drains out at the

289
00:15:30,607 --> 00:15:33,670
back, and the total stays at exactly one:

290
00:15:33,670 --> 00:15:36,832
here, to six decimal places, the whole time.

291
00:15:39,494 --> 00:15:43,806
A single plane wave has a perfectly definite momentum, but it's spread over

292
00:15:43,806 --> 00:15:45,106
all of space.

293
00:15:45,106 --> 00:15:49,056
To describe a particle that's somewhere, add plane waves together.

294
00:15:49,056 --> 00:15:53,244
Take wave numbers clustered around one value, with weights phi of k.

295
00:15:53,244 --> 00:15:55,306
One wave alone is flat.

296
00:15:55,306 --> 00:16:00,110
Add a few neighbors, and they reinforce in the middle and cancel elsewhere.

297
00:16:00,110 --> 00:16:03,885
With more and more components, the cancellation away from the center becomes

298
00:16:03,885 --> 00:16:06,573
complete, and we get a wave packet:

299
00:16:06,573 --> 00:16:10,998
a lump of probability with a hue that cycles at the central wave number.

300
00:16:10,998 --> 00:16:14,360
In the limit it's an integral, a Fourier transform.

301
00:16:14,360 --> 00:16:16,973
A narrow spread of k gives a wide packet;

302
00:16:16,973 --> 00:16:20,535
a wide spread gives a narrow one.

303
00:16:20,710 --> 00:16:22,660
Now let each component move.

304
00:16:22,660 --> 00:16:26,723
Put a single plane wave into the Schrödinger equation with no potential, and

305
00:16:26,723 --> 00:16:32,185
it works only if h-bar omega equals h-bar squared k squared over two m.

306
00:16:32,185 --> 00:16:35,710
So the frequency grows like the square of the wave number.

307
00:16:35,710 --> 00:16:40,123
This is the free particle's dispersion relation, and it means different components

308
00:16:40,123 --> 00:16:42,419
travel at different speeds.

309
00:16:42,419 --> 00:16:43,931
There are two speeds here.

310
00:16:43,931 --> 00:16:46,706
A single crest moves at omega over k:

311
00:16:46,706 --> 00:16:48,219
h-bar k over two m.

312
00:16:48,219 --> 00:16:52,531
But the lump, where the waves reinforce, moves at d omega d k:

313
00:16:52,531 --> 00:16:58,019
h-bar k over m, which is p over m, exactly the classical velocity.

314
00:16:58,019 --> 00:16:59,769
Watch two plane waves:

315
00:16:59,769 --> 00:17:03,481
the bright beats glide along at the group velocity, while the colors inside

316
00:17:03,481 --> 00:17:06,769
them crawl at half that speed.

317
00:17:08,510 --> 00:17:09,623
Now the real thing:

318
00:17:09,623 --> 00:17:12,398
a Gaussian packet, evolved exactly.

319
00:17:12,398 --> 00:17:16,348
The dashed line marks its center, moving at the group velocity.

320
00:17:16,348 --> 00:17:20,010
The white dot rides on one crest, one particular color.

321
00:17:20,010 --> 00:17:23,760
It moves too, but at half the speed, so the colors slip backward through the

322
00:17:23,760 --> 00:17:24,660
packet.

323
00:17:24,660 --> 00:17:28,335
The particle goes where the group goes.

324
00:17:29,535 --> 00:17:31,035
And the packet spreads.

325
00:17:31,035 --> 00:17:34,498
Its momentum content, on the right, never changes at all:

326
00:17:34,498 --> 00:17:36,998
a free particle keeps its momentum.

327
00:17:36,998 --> 00:17:41,423
The faster components simply run ahead and the slower ones fall behind.

328
00:17:41,423 --> 00:17:43,085
You can see it in the colors:

329
00:17:43,085 --> 00:17:45,985
the hue cycles faster at the front.

330
00:17:45,985 --> 00:17:46,948
How fast?

331
00:17:46,948 --> 00:17:50,960
A packet of width sigma zero has a momentum spread of h-bar over two sigma

332
00:17:50,960 --> 00:17:54,673
zero, a fact we'll derive with the uncertainty principle.

333
00:17:54,673 --> 00:17:58,710
Divide by the mass to get a spread of velocities, and at late times the width

334
00:17:58,710 --> 00:18:01,060
grows like that velocity spread times t.

335
00:18:01,060 --> 00:18:05,385
The exact law, from completing the square in the Fourier integral, is sigma

336
00:18:05,385 --> 00:18:10,998
zero times the square root of one plus h-bar t over two m sigma zero squared,

337
00:18:10,998 --> 00:18:12,510
all squared.

338
00:18:12,510 --> 00:18:17,735
The simulation sits right on it, to one part in ten thousand.

339
00:18:17,877 --> 00:18:22,565
The time scale for spreading is two m sigma zero squared over h-bar, so it

340
00:18:22,565 --> 00:18:24,840
depends enormously on mass.

341
00:18:24,840 --> 00:18:29,202
An electron squeezed into one angstrom, about the size of an atom, has spread

342
00:18:29,202 --> 00:18:32,302
to six angstroms after a single femtosecond.

343
00:18:32,302 --> 00:18:37,152
A one-microgram grain of dust, localized to a micron, would take about a million

344
00:18:37,152 --> 00:18:39,415
years just to double its width.

345
00:18:39,415 --> 00:18:42,477
That's one reason the everyday world looks so classical.

346
00:18:44,507 --> 00:18:46,645
Now let's put the equation to work.

347
00:18:46,645 --> 00:18:47,807
A standard trick:

348
00:18:47,807 --> 00:18:51,807
look for solutions that are a function of space times a function of time.

349
00:18:51,807 --> 00:18:54,445
Substitute, and divide by phi times T.

350
00:18:54,445 --> 00:18:59,207
The left side depends only on time, the right side only on position, so both

351
00:18:59,207 --> 00:19:01,345
must equal the same constant.

352
00:19:01,345 --> 00:19:02,699
Call it E.

353
00:19:02,699 --> 00:19:06,561
The time part is just e to the minus i E t over h-bar:

354
00:19:06,561 --> 00:19:09,724
a clock, turning at a rate set by the energy.

355
00:19:09,724 --> 00:19:13,361
The space part must satisfy H phi equals E phi.

356
00:19:13,361 --> 00:19:15,261
That's an eigenvalue problem:

357
00:19:15,261 --> 00:19:19,049
find the functions that H merely rescales, and the factors E.

358
00:19:19,049 --> 00:19:23,111
For these special solutions the probability density never changes, because

359
00:19:23,111 --> 00:19:25,374
a phase factor has length one.

360
00:19:25,374 --> 00:19:28,699
They're called stationary states.

361
00:19:29,199 --> 00:19:30,936
The simplest place to try it:

362
00:19:30,936 --> 00:19:35,349
a particle trapped in a box of length L, with walls it can never cross.

363
00:19:35,349 --> 00:19:40,336
Inside, there's no potential, so H phi equals E phi says phi double prime

364
00:19:40,336 --> 00:19:44,086
is minus two m E over h-bar squared times phi.

365
00:19:44,086 --> 00:19:46,786
At the walls the wavefunction must vanish.

366
00:19:46,786 --> 00:19:51,024
So start at the left wall at zero, pick a trial energy, and integrate across

367
00:19:51,024 --> 00:19:52,457
the box.

368
00:19:52,457 --> 00:19:56,782
For most energies, the wave arrives at the right wall at some nonzero height,

369
00:19:56,782 --> 00:19:59,032
and the boundary condition fails.

370
00:19:59,032 --> 00:20:02,420
Now sweep the energy upward, and track where the wave ends.

371
00:20:02,420 --> 00:20:05,945
Only at special energies does it land exactly on zero:

372
00:20:05,945 --> 00:20:09,332
whole numbers of half wavelengths fitting in the box, like the notes of a

373
00:20:09,332 --> 00:20:10,695
guitar string.

374
00:20:10,695 --> 00:20:12,720
That's where quantization comes from:

375
00:20:12,720 --> 00:20:18,232
not from a rule imposed by hand, but from a wave that must fit.

376
00:20:18,366 --> 00:20:22,553
The allowed solutions are sine waves with n half-wavelengths in the box, and

377
00:20:22,553 --> 00:20:24,728
their energies grow like n squared:

378
00:20:24,728 --> 00:20:28,128
one, four, nine, sixteen times the lowest.

379
00:20:28,128 --> 00:20:32,703
Here's the ladder of allowed energies, and the wave that belongs to each rung.

380
00:20:32,703 --> 00:20:34,728
The lowest energy isn't zero:

381
00:20:34,728 --> 00:20:38,028
a confined particle can never be perfectly at rest.

382
00:20:38,028 --> 00:20:44,266
For an electron in a box one nanometer wide, the lowest energy is 0.376 electron

383
00:20:44,266 --> 00:20:46,466
volts.

384
00:20:47,024 --> 00:20:49,824
Here are the first three, evolving in time.

385
00:20:49,824 --> 00:20:52,261
The density of each never changes.

386
00:20:52,261 --> 00:20:56,099
Only the colors turn, the whole wave at once, at rates proportional to the

387
00:20:56,099 --> 00:20:57,099
energy:

388
00:20:57,099 --> 00:21:03,474
the second turns four times faster than the first, the third nine times faster.

389
00:21:03,657 --> 00:21:06,670
Stationary doesn't mean the particle is sitting still.

390
00:21:06,670 --> 00:21:10,257
Measure the momentum of the second state and you'll find it's sharply peaked

391
00:21:10,257 --> 00:21:13,445
near plus or minus two pi h-bar over L:

392
00:21:13,445 --> 00:21:18,020
a standing wave is a sum of a wave moving right and one moving left.

393
00:21:18,020 --> 00:21:22,807
Their currents cancel exactly, so nothing flows, but the momentum is anything

394
00:21:22,807 --> 00:21:25,157
but zero.

395
00:21:25,324 --> 00:21:28,661
Things get interesting when we add two stationary states.

396
00:21:28,661 --> 00:21:32,624
Each part keeps turning at its own rate, so the angle between the two clocks

397
00:21:32,624 --> 00:21:35,461
keeps changing, and the two parts interfere:

398
00:21:35,461 --> 00:21:39,449
constructively on the left, then on the right, then on the left again.

399
00:21:39,449 --> 00:21:42,632
The probability sloshes back and forth.

400
00:21:42,632 --> 00:21:44,557
The algebra says the same.

401
00:21:44,557 --> 00:21:48,920
Square the sum, and alongside the two static densities there's a cross term

402
00:21:48,920 --> 00:21:53,282
oscillating at the difference of the two energies, divided by h-bar.

403
00:21:53,282 --> 00:21:57,545
So the average position swings back and forth with amplitude sixteen L over

404
00:21:57,545 --> 00:22:01,345
nine pi squared, about eighteen percent of the box.

405
00:22:01,345 --> 00:22:07,524
For an electron in a nanometer box, that's one swing every 3.67 femtoseconds.

406
00:22:07,524 --> 00:22:08,686
Remember this:

407
00:22:08,686 --> 00:22:12,874
a superposition of two energies oscillates at the difference frequency, E

408
00:22:12,874 --> 00:22:15,874
two minus E one over h-bar.

409
00:22:15,874 --> 00:22:19,849
These are called Bohr frequencies, and when we reach the hydrogen atom they'll

410
00:22:19,849 --> 00:22:22,399
turn out to be the colors of the light it emits.

411
00:22:24,053 --> 00:22:25,953
Let's push that idea further.

412
00:22:25,953 --> 00:22:28,641
Put a narrow packet in the box, off center.

413
00:22:28,641 --> 00:22:32,378
Like any wavefunction in the box, it's a sum of the stationary states, with

414
00:22:32,378 --> 00:22:33,353
coefficients c n.

415
00:22:33,353 --> 00:22:35,491
Here are their sizes squared.

416
00:22:35,491 --> 00:22:39,278
Every term carries its own clock, and the clocks run at rates proportional

417
00:22:39,278 --> 00:22:40,478
to n squared:

418
00:22:40,478 --> 00:22:45,178
one, four, nine, sixteen, and so on.

419
00:22:46,353 --> 00:22:48,766
Give it a push to the right and let it go.

420
00:22:48,766 --> 00:22:50,791
At first it behaves like a ball:

421
00:22:50,791 --> 00:22:55,128
it moves, spreads a little, bounces off the wall and comes back.

422
00:22:55,128 --> 00:22:58,953
But the clocks drift out of step, and within a couple of bounces the packet

423
00:22:58,953 --> 00:23:02,653
has smeared into a tangle of ripples filling the whole box.

424
00:23:02,653 --> 00:23:07,553
It looks like the information about where it started is gone for good.

425
00:23:08,253 --> 00:23:11,291
To see the whole story at once, stack the snapshots:

426
00:23:11,291 --> 00:23:15,153
x across, time going down, brightness for the probability.

427
00:23:15,153 --> 00:23:17,495
We'll weave it one row at a time.

428
00:23:17,495 --> 00:23:20,007
The early zigzag is the bouncing packet.

429
00:23:20,007 --> 00:23:24,170
Then it dissolves into a fine weave, crossed by straight dark channels...

430
00:23:24,170 --> 00:23:25,932
but look at a quarter of the way through:

431
00:23:25,932 --> 00:23:30,470
two clean packets, one where we started and one at the mirror-image spot.

432
00:23:30,470 --> 00:23:34,095
At half the period, a single packet again, perfectly mirrored.

433
00:23:34,095 --> 00:23:38,332
And at the end of the period, the original packet, exactly as it began.

434
00:23:38,332 --> 00:23:41,270
Then the whole pattern repeats forever.

435
00:23:41,270 --> 00:23:43,607
Nothing here was arranged by hand.

436
00:23:43,607 --> 00:23:47,820
This intricate pattern, called a quantum carpet, is just three hundred sine

437
00:23:47,820 --> 00:23:51,945
waves, each turning at a rate proportional to n squared.

438
00:23:51,945 --> 00:23:56,495
It's the same mathematics as an optical effect Henry Fox Talbot noticed in

439
00:23:56,495 --> 00:24:01,520
1836, when light passes through a grating.

440
00:24:01,653 --> 00:24:02,828
Why does it come back?

441
00:24:02,828 --> 00:24:06,041
Each clock turns at n squared times the slowest rate.

442
00:24:06,041 --> 00:24:10,291
After one full turn of the slowest clock, the n-th clock has made exactly

443
00:24:10,291 --> 00:24:11,891
n squared turns.

444
00:24:11,891 --> 00:24:16,028
Every n squared is a whole number, so every clock is back where it started,

445
00:24:16,028 --> 00:24:18,091
and so is the wavefunction.

446
00:24:18,091 --> 00:24:21,703
The revival time is two pi h-bar over E one:

447
00:24:21,703 --> 00:24:24,895
four m L squared over pi h-bar.

448
00:24:24,895 --> 00:24:29,107
At half the period, each clock is at plus or minus one, depending on whether

449
00:24:29,107 --> 00:24:33,832
n is even or odd, and that sign pattern is exactly what reflects a sine series

450
00:24:33,832 --> 00:24:35,407
about the middle of the box:

451
00:24:35,407 --> 00:24:36,895
a mirror image.

452
00:24:36,895 --> 00:24:41,057
At a quarter period the even and odd terms pick up different phases, and the

453
00:24:41,057 --> 00:24:46,107
wave splits into the packet plus its mirror image, each with half the probability:

454
00:24:46,107 --> 00:24:51,270
a particle in a superposition of two places, produced by nothing but waiting.

455
00:24:51,270 --> 00:24:56,720
For an electron in a one-nanometer box, the full revival takes eleven femtoseconds.

456
00:24:58,800 --> 00:25:02,338
Now a question classical physics answers instantly.

457
00:25:02,338 --> 00:25:07,475
A particle with energy E approaches a wall of potential energy V zero, higher

458
00:25:07,475 --> 00:25:07,950
than E.

459
00:25:07,950 --> 00:25:11,800
It doesn't have enough energy to be inside the wall at all, so it must turn

460
00:25:11,800 --> 00:25:12,863
around.

461
00:25:12,863 --> 00:25:15,375
Every time.

462
00:25:15,517 --> 00:25:19,879
Quantum mechanically, look at the energy equation inside the barrier, where

463
00:25:19,879 --> 00:25:21,429
V zero is bigger than E.

464
00:25:21,429 --> 00:25:26,017
Now the second derivative has the same sign as phi itself, so the solutions

465
00:25:26,017 --> 00:25:27,579
aren't waves at all.

466
00:25:27,579 --> 00:25:31,979
They're growing and decaying exponentials, with a decay rate kappa set by

467
00:25:31,979 --> 00:25:34,817
how far the energy falls short.

468
00:25:34,817 --> 00:25:38,229
So here's the exact solution for a steady stream of particles coming from

469
00:25:38,229 --> 00:25:39,229
the left.

470
00:25:39,229 --> 00:25:43,517
Inside the barrier, the wave decays exponentially, but it doesn't reach zero

471
00:25:43,517 --> 00:25:45,017
before the far side.

472
00:25:45,017 --> 00:25:47,917
At each edge, the pieces must join smoothly:

473
00:25:47,917 --> 00:25:50,217
the wave and its slope continuous.

474
00:25:50,217 --> 00:25:53,592
So on the far side, a smaller wave carries on.

475
00:25:53,592 --> 00:25:57,717
On the near side, the reflected wave interferes with the incoming one, making

476
00:25:57,717 --> 00:26:00,167
these ripples.

477
00:26:00,775 --> 00:26:05,050
Matching the pieces at both edges gives four linear equations, and solving

478
00:26:05,050 --> 00:26:08,250
them gives the transmission probability exactly.

479
00:26:08,250 --> 00:26:11,763
It involves a hyperbolic sine of kappa times the width.

480
00:26:11,763 --> 00:26:15,363
For a thick barrier that's dominated by a single factor:

481
00:26:15,363 --> 00:26:17,100
e to the minus two kappa a.

482
00:26:17,100 --> 00:26:21,925
Exponential in the width, and in the square root of the energy deficit.

483
00:26:21,925 --> 00:26:23,513
Here's the whole curve.

484
00:26:23,513 --> 00:26:27,838
Below the top of the barrier, transmission is small but never zero.

485
00:26:27,838 --> 00:26:32,113
Above the top, where a classical particle would always get through, a quantum

486
00:26:32,113 --> 00:26:36,438
one is sometimes reflected, except at special energies where a whole number

487
00:26:36,438 --> 00:26:41,900
of half-waves fits across the barrier and the reflections cancel.

488
00:26:42,509 --> 00:26:48,321
Now send in a wave packet, simulated by solving the Schrödinger equation numerically.

489
00:26:48,321 --> 00:26:51,934
The packet's average energy is below the top of the barrier.

490
00:26:51,934 --> 00:26:54,046
It hits, and splits in two:

491
00:26:54,046 --> 00:26:59,759
most of it reflects, but part of it appears on the far side and keeps going.

492
00:27:01,967 --> 00:27:06,154
In the end, 14.16 percent of the probability got through.

493
00:27:06,154 --> 00:27:11,317
The formula, averaged over the packet's spread of energies, predicts 14.18

494
00:27:11,317 --> 00:27:12,342
percent.

495
00:27:12,342 --> 00:27:16,629
Notice that's more than the 13.1 percent you'd get by plugging in the average

496
00:27:16,629 --> 00:27:18,017
energy alone.

497
00:27:18,017 --> 00:27:21,217
That difference is a clue.

498
00:27:21,438 --> 00:27:23,200
Look at the momentum of each part.

499
00:27:23,200 --> 00:27:25,888
Here's the incoming packet's spread of momenta.

500
00:27:25,888 --> 00:27:29,013
The part that got through is shifted toward higher momentum:

501
00:27:29,013 --> 00:27:31,588
the faster components tunnel more easily.

502
00:27:31,588 --> 00:27:35,538
Its average energy is about seven percent higher than the packet's, and the

503
00:27:35,538 --> 00:27:38,100
reflected part is slightly slower.

504
00:27:38,100 --> 00:27:41,025
So a particle doesn't lose energy by tunneling.

505
00:27:41,025 --> 00:27:45,388
The barrier acts as a filter that preferentially lets the more energetic components

506
00:27:45,388 --> 00:27:47,488
through.

507
00:27:48,063 --> 00:27:52,113
That exponential makes tunneling extremely sensitive to distance.

508
00:27:52,113 --> 00:27:56,413
For an electron one electron volt short of the top, kappa is about five per

509
00:27:56,413 --> 00:27:57,600
nanometer.

510
00:27:57,600 --> 00:28:01,300
Through half a nanometer, roughly six in a thousand get through;

511
00:28:01,300 --> 00:28:05,213
through one nanometer, about four in a hundred thousand.

512
00:28:05,213 --> 00:28:07,275
That sensitivity is useful.

513
00:28:07,275 --> 00:28:11,813
A scanning tunneling microscope holds a sharp tip just above a surface;

514
00:28:11,813 --> 00:28:15,963
with a typical work function, the tunneling current changes about ninefold

515
00:28:15,963 --> 00:28:20,225
for every angstrom of gap, enough to map individual atoms.

516
00:28:20,225 --> 00:28:25,013
Tunneling is how alpha particles escape from nuclei, as Gamow explained in

517
00:28:25,013 --> 00:28:26,738
1928.

518
00:28:26,738 --> 00:28:31,900
And the 2025 Nobel Prize in physics went to experiments showing that a whole

519
00:28:31,900 --> 00:28:36,188
superconducting circuit, with vast numbers of electrons moving together as

520
00:28:36,188 --> 00:28:38,063
one, can tunnel too.

521
00:28:43,680 --> 00:28:46,093
Step back and look at what we've been doing.

522
00:28:46,093 --> 00:28:50,268
A wavefunction is a list of complex numbers, one for each point.

523
00:28:50,268 --> 00:28:55,030
Sample it at N points, and it literally is a vector with N complex components.

524
00:28:55,030 --> 00:28:58,243
Shrink the spacing, and N goes to infinity.

525
00:28:58,243 --> 00:29:00,818
That's the right way to think about a quantum state:

526
00:29:00,818 --> 00:29:04,889
as a vector, written with this angle bracket, called a ket.

527
00:29:04,889 --> 00:29:06,764
Vectors have dot products.

528
00:29:06,764 --> 00:29:11,714
For complex vectors you conjugate the first one, multiply component by component,

529
00:29:11,714 --> 00:29:12,701
and add.

530
00:29:12,701 --> 00:29:15,264
In the limit, the sum becomes an integral:

531
00:29:15,264 --> 00:29:17,814
the inner product of two wavefunctions.

532
00:29:17,814 --> 00:29:21,851
And the length squared of psi is exactly the total probability.

533
00:29:21,851 --> 00:29:25,589
A quantum state is a unit vector.

534
00:29:26,055 --> 00:29:29,993
The stationary states of the box make a natural set of axes.

535
00:29:29,993 --> 00:29:32,830
Multiply two different ones together and integrate:

536
00:29:32,830 --> 00:29:36,255
the positive and negative areas cancel exactly.

537
00:29:36,255 --> 00:29:39,168
Multiply one by itself and you get one.

538
00:29:39,168 --> 00:29:40,330
They're orthonormal:

539
00:29:40,330 --> 00:29:45,480
perpendicular unit vectors, in infinitely many dimensions.

540
00:29:45,664 --> 00:29:49,789
Any wavefunction in the box can be written as a combination of these axes,

541
00:29:49,789 --> 00:29:53,414
and the coefficient along each axis is just the inner product:

542
00:29:53,414 --> 00:29:55,801
the shadow of psi on that axis.

543
00:29:55,801 --> 00:29:59,276
Adding the terms one by one rebuilds our narrow packet:

544
00:29:59,276 --> 00:30:03,847
one term, two, four, eight, sixteen, thirty-two.

545
00:30:03,847 --> 00:30:07,934
And since the axes are perpendicular, the squared lengths of the shadows add

546
00:30:07,934 --> 00:30:11,109
up to the squared length of the vector, which is one.

547
00:30:11,109 --> 00:30:15,559
That's Pythagoras' theorem, in a space with infinitely many dimensions.

548
00:30:15,559 --> 00:30:20,272
This space of wavefunctions is called a Hilbert space.

549
00:30:20,397 --> 00:30:24,397
If states are vectors, then the Hamiltonian, which acts on states, must be

550
00:30:24,397 --> 00:30:25,534
a matrix.

551
00:30:25,534 --> 00:30:26,722
Let's build it.

552
00:30:26,722 --> 00:30:30,722
On a grid, the second derivative at one point is the neighbors minus twice

553
00:30:30,722 --> 00:30:33,647
the point itself, over the spacing squared.

554
00:30:33,647 --> 00:30:38,672
So minus h-bar squared over two m times the second derivative is this matrix:

555
00:30:38,672 --> 00:30:43,672
twos on the diagonal, minus ones beside it, zeros everywhere else.

556
00:30:43,672 --> 00:30:46,597
Hand this matrix to any linear-algebra routine.

557
00:30:46,597 --> 00:30:50,984
Its eigenvectors come out as exactly the sampled sine waves of the box, and

558
00:30:50,984 --> 00:30:56,284
as the grid gets finer, its eigenvalues approach one, four, nine, sixteen,

559
00:30:56,284 --> 00:30:58,434
twenty-five times the lowest.

560
00:30:58,434 --> 00:31:02,659
The energy levels of a quantum system are the eigenvalues of a matrix.

561
00:31:02,659 --> 00:31:06,134
That's the sense in which Heisenberg's matrix mechanics and Schrödinger's

562
00:31:06,134 --> 00:31:13,772
wave mechanics, invented separately in 1925 and 1926, are the same theory.

563
00:31:14,280 --> 00:31:19,255
Our matrix is symmetric, and for complex matrices the right notion is Hermitian:

564
00:31:19,255 --> 00:31:23,518
equal to its own conjugate transpose, which means H can be moved from one

565
00:31:23,518 --> 00:31:25,855
side of an inner product to the other.

566
00:31:25,855 --> 00:31:27,605
Two short consequences.

567
00:31:27,605 --> 00:31:28,418
First:

568
00:31:28,418 --> 00:31:33,268
if phi is an eigenvector with eigenvalue E, move H across the inner product

569
00:31:33,268 --> 00:31:36,693
and you find E equals its own complex conjugate.

570
00:31:36,693 --> 00:31:40,780
Energies are real numbers, as measured quantities should be.

571
00:31:40,780 --> 00:31:41,305
Second:

572
00:31:41,305 --> 00:31:45,405
for two eigenvectors with different eigenvalues, the same move shows that

573
00:31:45,405 --> 00:31:47,730
their inner product must be zero.

574
00:31:47,730 --> 00:31:49,655
They're perpendicular.

575
00:31:49,655 --> 00:31:52,168
Together these give the spectral theorem:

576
00:31:52,168 --> 00:31:57,455
a Hermitian operator is completely described by its eigenvalues and its perpendicular

577
00:31:57,455 --> 00:31:58,905
eigenvectors.

578
00:31:58,905 --> 00:32:05,280
Here, H is a sum of energies times projections onto the stationary states.

579
00:32:05,805 --> 00:32:09,455
And time evolution has the cleanest possible form in this basis.

580
00:32:09,455 --> 00:32:14,455
The evolution operator, e to the minus i H t over h-bar, just multiplies each

581
00:32:14,455 --> 00:32:16,643
coefficient by its own phase.

582
00:32:16,643 --> 00:32:20,443
Each coefficient is a clock running at its own rate, and since phases don't

583
00:32:20,443 --> 00:32:23,868
change lengths, the sum of the squares stays one forever.

584
00:32:23,868 --> 00:32:26,693
The state vector rotates in Hilbert space.

585
00:32:26,693 --> 00:32:30,643
Everything we saw in the box, the sloshing and the carpet, is this:

586
00:32:30,643 --> 00:32:32,205
a set of clocks, summed.

587
00:32:35,119 --> 00:32:38,181
We can now state the rules of the whole theory on one screen.

588
00:32:38,181 --> 00:32:41,531
A state is a unit vector, up to an overall phase.

589
00:32:41,531 --> 00:32:45,794
Every measurable quantity is a Hermitian operator, described by its eigenvalues

590
00:32:45,794 --> 00:32:47,306
and eigenvectors.

591
00:32:47,306 --> 00:32:51,244
A measurement gives one of the eigenvalues, at random, with probability equal

592
00:32:51,244 --> 00:32:54,394
to the squared length of the state's shadow on that eigenvector:

593
00:32:54,394 --> 00:32:55,819
the Born rule.

594
00:32:55,819 --> 00:32:59,194
Right after the measurement, the state is that eigenvector.

595
00:32:59,194 --> 00:33:04,519
And between measurements, the state evolves by the Schrödinger equation.

596
00:33:05,048 --> 00:33:09,110
Picture it with just three dimensions, and real numbers so we can draw it.

597
00:33:09,110 --> 00:33:13,173
The three perpendicular axes are the eigenvectors of some observable, with

598
00:33:13,173 --> 00:33:15,810
outcomes a one, a two, a three.

599
00:33:15,810 --> 00:33:19,248
The state is a unit vector pointing somewhere in between.

600
00:33:19,248 --> 00:33:23,898
Its shadows on the axes, squared, are the probabilities of the three outcomes,

601
00:33:23,898 --> 00:33:26,435
and by Pythagoras they add up to one.

602
00:33:26,435 --> 00:33:28,485
That's why the Born rule squares:

603
00:33:28,485 --> 00:33:31,948
it's the only way to turn the components of a unit vector into probabilities

604
00:33:31,948 --> 00:33:34,206
that always sum to one.

605
00:33:34,206 --> 00:33:35,244
Now measure.

606
00:33:35,244 --> 00:33:37,431
Suppose the outcome is a two.

607
00:33:37,431 --> 00:33:39,719
The state jumps onto that axis:

608
00:33:39,719 --> 00:33:43,006
measure again immediately and you're certain to get a two.

609
00:33:43,006 --> 00:33:46,931
That jump is the one part of the theory that isn't described by the Schrödinger

610
00:33:46,931 --> 00:33:50,731
equation, and what really happens in it is still debated.

611
00:33:50,731 --> 00:33:55,406
But the rule itself has been tested to great precision.

612
00:33:55,598 --> 00:33:58,998
Let's measure the energy of our narrow packet in the box.

613
00:33:58,998 --> 00:34:03,498
The possible outcomes are the energies E n, with probabilities given by the

614
00:34:03,498 --> 00:34:06,235
squared coefficients we computed before.

615
00:34:06,235 --> 00:34:08,535
Each measurement gives just one of them.

616
00:34:08,535 --> 00:34:13,060
Here are two thousand simulated measurements on two thousand identical copies

617
00:34:13,060 --> 00:34:14,148
of the state.

618
00:34:14,148 --> 00:34:18,490
The fractions settle onto the predicted probabilities.

619
00:34:18,490 --> 00:34:21,527
And each individual measurement changes the state.

620
00:34:21,527 --> 00:34:23,965
Here the first outcome was E seven:

621
00:34:23,965 --> 00:34:28,190
the packet is replaced by the seventh stationary state, and stays there.

622
00:34:28,190 --> 00:34:30,840
Prepare a fresh packet and measure again.

623
00:34:30,840 --> 00:34:31,777
This time:

624
00:34:31,777 --> 00:34:35,315
E four, and a different collapse.

625
00:34:35,448 --> 00:34:39,223
With the probabilities in hand, the average outcome follows.

626
00:34:39,223 --> 00:34:42,448
It's the sum of each outcome times its probability.

627
00:34:42,448 --> 00:34:47,198
Write the probability as the shadow times its conjugate, pull the sum inside,

628
00:34:47,198 --> 00:34:49,448
and the operator itself appears:

629
00:34:49,448 --> 00:34:52,160
the average of A is psi, A psi.

630
00:34:52,160 --> 00:34:55,640
You never need the eigenvectors to compute an average.

631
00:34:55,640 --> 00:34:56,827
For our packet:

632
00:34:56,827 --> 00:35:01,752
summing energies times probabilities gives 484.73.

633
00:35:01,752 --> 00:35:06,452
Computing psi, H psi directly, as an integral of the slope squared, gives

634
00:35:06,452 --> 00:35:09,640
the same 484.73.

635
00:35:09,640 --> 00:35:15,590
And the average of our two thousand random outcomes is 487.1, within the noise

636
00:35:15,590 --> 00:35:19,440
you'd expect from two thousand samples.

637
00:35:19,973 --> 00:35:22,273
The same rules cover every observable;

638
00:35:22,273 --> 00:35:24,098
only the axes change.

639
00:35:24,098 --> 00:35:26,973
For energy, the axes are the stationary states.

640
00:35:26,973 --> 00:35:30,535
For position, they're infinitely narrow spikes, and the shadow on the spike

641
00:35:30,535 --> 00:35:32,973
at x zero is just psi of x zero.

642
00:35:32,973 --> 00:35:37,148
So Born's rule for position, from the start of the video, is a special case.

643
00:35:37,148 --> 00:35:41,235
For momentum, the axes are plane waves, and the shadow is a new function,

644
00:35:41,235 --> 00:35:41,873
phi of p.

645
00:35:41,873 --> 00:35:45,085
What is it, and how are these two sets of axes related?

646
00:35:45,085 --> 00:35:46,048
That's next.

647
00:35:47,975 --> 00:35:51,512
The momentum operator is minus i h-bar times d by d x.

648
00:35:51,512 --> 00:35:53,912
Its eigenvectors are the plane waves:

649
00:35:53,912 --> 00:35:58,350
differentiating e to the i p x over h-bar just brings down a factor of p.

650
00:35:58,350 --> 00:36:01,712
Normalized, these are the axes for momentum.

651
00:36:01,712 --> 00:36:06,187
So the amplitude to find momentum p, the shadow of psi on each plane wave,

652
00:36:06,187 --> 00:36:07,375
is this integral:

653
00:36:07,375 --> 00:36:09,137
the Fourier transform.

654
00:36:09,137 --> 00:36:13,500
Position and momentum are two sets of axes for the same vector, related by

655
00:36:13,500 --> 00:36:16,500
a Fourier transform.

656
00:36:17,066 --> 00:36:20,091
Watch the two pictures together for a Gaussian packet.

657
00:36:20,091 --> 00:36:24,091
Squeeze it in position, and its momentum distribution spreads out.

658
00:36:24,091 --> 00:36:27,979
Spread it in position, and the momentum distribution sharpens.

659
00:36:27,979 --> 00:36:31,616
For a Gaussian, the product of the two widths never changes:

660
00:36:31,616 --> 00:36:34,004
it's always h-bar over two.

661
00:36:34,004 --> 00:36:37,791
That's a hint of the uncertainty principle, and it's a property of Fourier

662
00:36:37,791 --> 00:36:41,941
transforms, not of any measuring device.

663
00:36:42,075 --> 00:36:45,662
Here's the single most important equation in quantum mechanics.

664
00:36:45,662 --> 00:36:49,912
Apply position and momentum to a test function f, in both orders.

665
00:36:49,912 --> 00:36:51,912
Momentum first, then position:

666
00:36:51,912 --> 00:36:54,312
minus i h-bar x f prime.

667
00:36:54,312 --> 00:36:56,500
Position first, then momentum:

668
00:36:56,500 --> 00:36:59,950
the product rule gives an extra term, minus i h-bar f.

669
00:36:59,950 --> 00:37:04,491
Subtract, and everything cancels except i h-bar times f.

670
00:37:04,491 --> 00:37:08,891
Since f was arbitrary, x p minus p x equals i h-bar:

671
00:37:08,891 --> 00:37:11,391
the canonical commutation relation.

672
00:37:11,391 --> 00:37:15,904
Born and Jordan found it in 1925, in the matrix version of the theory, and

673
00:37:15,904 --> 00:37:18,516
it's carved on Max Born's gravestone.

674
00:37:18,516 --> 00:37:22,154
It says position and momentum don't share any eigenvectors:

675
00:37:22,154 --> 00:37:25,316
nothing is both a spike in x and a plane wave in p.

676
00:37:25,316 --> 00:37:28,991
Almost everything that's strange about quantum mechanics flows from this one

677
00:37:28,991 --> 00:37:33,241
line, starting with the uncertainty principle.

678
00:37:33,758 --> 00:37:36,371
There's a deeper way to see what momentum is.

679
00:37:36,371 --> 00:37:37,771
Exponentiate it:

680
00:37:37,771 --> 00:37:40,546
e to the minus i a p over h-bar.

681
00:37:40,546 --> 00:37:45,108
Expand the exponential as a power series, and since p is a derivative, you

682
00:37:45,108 --> 00:37:48,921
get exactly Taylor's series for psi evaluated at x minus a.

683
00:37:48,921 --> 00:37:53,721
Watch the partial sums of that series, using only derivatives at each point,

684
00:37:53,721 --> 00:37:55,208
march the function over by a.

685
00:37:55,208 --> 00:38:00,208
Momentum is what generates translations in space.

686
00:38:00,325 --> 00:38:04,037
And commutators also tell us how things change in time.

687
00:38:04,037 --> 00:38:07,025
Differentiate an average with the product rule.

688
00:38:07,025 --> 00:38:11,612
Use the Schrödinger equation for each time derivative, move H across, since

689
00:38:11,612 --> 00:38:16,737
it's Hermitian, and the rate of change of any average is i over h-bar times

690
00:38:16,737 --> 00:38:20,158
the average of the commutator of H with A.

691
00:38:20,158 --> 00:38:23,458
So anything that commutes with the Hamiltonian is conserved.

692
00:38:23,458 --> 00:38:28,283
For a free particle, H is p squared over two m, which commutes with p, so

693
00:38:28,283 --> 00:38:30,196
momentum is conserved:

694
00:38:30,196 --> 00:38:34,696
exactly why the momentum distribution of our spreading packet never changed.

695
00:38:34,696 --> 00:38:39,183
The Hamiltonian doesn't care where the particle is, so momentum, the generator

696
00:38:39,183 --> 00:38:41,721
of translations, can't change.

697
00:38:41,721 --> 00:38:44,033
Symmetries give conservation laws.

698
00:38:45,981 --> 00:38:47,793
We've seen hints of it twice now.

699
00:38:47,793 --> 00:38:50,818
Squeeze a packet in position and its momentum spreads.

700
00:38:50,818 --> 00:38:52,356
Here's the precise statement:

701
00:38:52,356 --> 00:38:56,143
the standard deviation of position times the standard deviation of momentum

702
00:38:56,143 --> 00:38:58,318
is at least h-bar over two.

703
00:38:58,318 --> 00:39:01,168
Heisenberg had the idea in 1927;

704
00:39:01,168 --> 00:39:03,768
Kennard proved this exact form the same year;

705
00:39:03,768 --> 00:39:06,306
Robertson generalized it two years later.

706
00:39:06,306 --> 00:39:09,018
And it's worth being careful about what it says.

707
00:39:09,018 --> 00:39:13,731
The sigmas are spreads of outcomes, over many copies of the same state.

708
00:39:13,731 --> 00:39:16,456
It isn't about measurements disturbing things.

709
00:39:16,456 --> 00:39:22,031
It's a property of the state itself, and we can now prove it in a few lines.

710
00:39:22,585 --> 00:39:26,110
The proof rests on one geometric fact about vectors.

711
00:39:26,110 --> 00:39:30,747
The inner product of f with g is the length of f times the length of g's shadow

712
00:39:30,747 --> 00:39:31,597
on it.

713
00:39:31,597 --> 00:39:35,535
A shadow is never longer than the vector casting it, so the inner product

714
00:39:35,535 --> 00:39:38,847
squared is at most the product of the two lengths squared.

715
00:39:38,847 --> 00:39:43,735
That's the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, and it holds in any Hilbert space.

716
00:39:43,735 --> 00:39:48,285
It's an equality only when the two vectors are parallel.

717
00:39:48,443 --> 00:39:51,806
Take any two observables A and B, and a state psi.

718
00:39:51,806 --> 00:39:53,306
Define two vectors:

719
00:39:53,306 --> 00:39:57,193
f is A minus its average, acting on psi, and g is the same for B.

720
00:39:57,193 --> 00:40:00,381
Their squared lengths are exactly the two variances.

721
00:40:00,381 --> 00:40:04,193
By Cauchy-Schwarz, the product of the variances is at least the inner product

722
00:40:04,193 --> 00:40:05,156
squared.

723
00:40:05,156 --> 00:40:09,581
A complex number's size squared is at least its imaginary part squared, and

724
00:40:09,581 --> 00:40:13,693
the imaginary part is the difference between the inner product and its conjugate,

725
00:40:13,693 --> 00:40:15,452
over two i.

726
00:40:15,452 --> 00:40:17,502
Multiply out that difference.

727
00:40:17,502 --> 00:40:22,202
The averages cancel, and what's left is the average of A B minus B A:

728
00:40:22,202 --> 00:40:23,464
the commutator.

729
00:40:23,464 --> 00:40:24,852
Take a square root:

730
00:40:24,852 --> 00:40:29,152
the product of the spreads is at least half the size of the average commutator.

731
00:40:29,152 --> 00:40:34,502
That's Robertson's uncertainty relation, for any pair of observables at all.

732
00:40:34,502 --> 00:40:37,127
Now plug in position and momentum.

733
00:40:37,127 --> 00:40:42,764
Their commutator is i h-bar, a constant, so its average is i h-bar in every

734
00:40:42,764 --> 00:40:43,689
state.

735
00:40:43,689 --> 00:40:46,377
Half its size is h-bar over two.

736
00:40:46,377 --> 00:40:50,152
Notice that no measuring device appears anywhere in this proof.

737
00:40:50,152 --> 00:40:55,177
The uncertainty principle is a consequence of the commutator.

738
00:40:55,752 --> 00:40:57,989
When is the bound reached exactly?

739
00:40:57,989 --> 00:41:00,139
Both inequalities must be tight:

740
00:41:00,139 --> 00:41:04,289
g must be parallel to f, with a purely imaginary factor.

741
00:41:04,289 --> 00:41:08,464
For position and momentum, that's a first-order differential equation, and

742
00:41:08,464 --> 00:41:10,502
its solution is a Gaussian.

743
00:41:10,502 --> 00:41:14,952
So Gaussian packets, the ones we've been using all along, are the least uncertain

744
00:41:14,952 --> 00:41:17,527
states there are.

745
00:41:18,068 --> 00:41:19,306
Let's map it out.

746
00:41:19,306 --> 00:41:23,043
Here's the plane of position spread against momentum spread, on logarithmic

747
00:41:23,043 --> 00:41:28,068
axes, so the bound becomes a straight line, and everything below it is forbidden.

748
00:41:28,068 --> 00:41:31,093
Gaussians of every width sit exactly on the line.

749
00:41:31,093 --> 00:41:33,493
The box's stationary states sit above it:

750
00:41:33,493 --> 00:41:38,318
the lowest at 0.568 h-bar, the others higher and higher.

751
00:41:38,318 --> 00:41:42,443
The harmonic oscillator's states, which we'll meet next, sit at n plus one

752
00:41:42,443 --> 00:41:44,518
half times h-bar.

753
00:41:44,518 --> 00:41:48,606
And a free Gaussian packet, as it spreads, slides away from the line:

754
00:41:48,606 --> 00:41:52,806
its position spread grows while its momentum spread stays fixed.

755
00:41:52,806 --> 00:41:54,631
So does it become more uncertain?

756
00:41:54,631 --> 00:41:56,193
In one sense, yes.

757
00:41:56,193 --> 00:41:59,718
But something subtler is going on.

758
00:42:00,202 --> 00:42:04,002
In the derivation we threw away the real part of the inner product.

759
00:42:04,002 --> 00:42:07,702
Schrödinger kept it in 1930, and got a stronger bound:

760
00:42:07,702 --> 00:42:11,839
the extra term is the covariance between position and momentum.

761
00:42:11,839 --> 00:42:16,089
For our spreading packet, the covariance grows, because the faster components

762
00:42:16,089 --> 00:42:20,427
are out in front, and the product of the variances minus the covariance squared

763
00:42:20,427 --> 00:42:25,977
stays at exactly one quarter, h-bar squared over four, at every moment.

764
00:42:25,977 --> 00:42:30,614
The spreading packet never gets any less sharp than quantum mechanics allows;

765
00:42:30,614 --> 00:42:33,102
its uncertainty just becomes correlated.

766
00:42:38,811 --> 00:42:41,261
Next, the most important system in physics:

767
00:42:41,261 --> 00:42:42,874
the harmonic oscillator.

768
00:42:42,874 --> 00:42:44,286
Why so important?

769
00:42:44,286 --> 00:42:47,511
Take any potential with a smooth minimum, and zoom in.

770
00:42:47,511 --> 00:42:50,699
Near the bottom, Taylor's theorem says it's a parabola.

771
00:42:50,699 --> 00:42:54,899
So the oscillator describes anything that vibrates a little around equilibrium:

772
00:42:54,899 --> 00:42:58,261
molecules, crystals, even the modes of light.

773
00:42:58,261 --> 00:43:05,211
Its Hamiltonian is p squared over two m plus one half m omega squared x squared.

774
00:43:05,661 --> 00:43:07,286
Dirac's trick for solving it:

775
00:43:07,286 --> 00:43:09,036
factor the Hamiltonian.

776
00:43:09,036 --> 00:43:13,386
For ordinary numbers, u squared plus v squared factors as u minus i v times

777
00:43:13,386 --> 00:43:14,286
u plus i v.

778
00:43:14,286 --> 00:43:19,174
So define the operator a, a combination of x and i p, and its adjoint, a dagger,

779
00:43:19,174 --> 00:43:20,799
with the opposite sign.

780
00:43:20,799 --> 00:43:24,349
Multiply them in this order and the cross terms don't quite cancel, because

781
00:43:24,349 --> 00:43:26,224
x and p don't commute.

782
00:43:26,224 --> 00:43:30,524
What's left is the commutator, i h-bar, which turns into a constant:

783
00:43:30,524 --> 00:43:32,303
minus one half.

784
00:43:32,303 --> 00:43:37,403
So the Hamiltonian is h-bar omega times a dagger a, plus one half.

785
00:43:37,403 --> 00:43:41,828
And the same commutator gives a times a dagger minus a dagger times a equals

786
00:43:41,828 --> 00:43:42,690
one.

787
00:43:42,690 --> 00:43:45,990
That half and that one are the fingerprints of i h-bar.

788
00:43:45,990 --> 00:43:49,628
Everything else follows from algebra.

789
00:43:50,253 --> 00:43:52,040
Now the key commutators.

790
00:43:52,040 --> 00:43:56,553
H with a dagger gives h-bar omega a dagger, and H with a gives minus h-bar

791
00:43:56,553 --> 00:43:57,165
omega a.

792
00:43:57,165 --> 00:43:58,240
Their meaning:

793
00:43:58,240 --> 00:44:03,140
if psi has energy E, then a dagger psi has energy E plus h-bar omega, and

794
00:44:03,140 --> 00:44:06,465
a psi has energy E minus h-bar omega.

795
00:44:06,465 --> 00:44:08,090
They're ladder operators:

796
00:44:08,090 --> 00:44:12,815
a dagger climbs one rung, a descends one, and the rungs are evenly spaced,

797
00:44:12,815 --> 00:44:15,111
h-bar omega apart.

798
00:44:15,111 --> 00:44:17,311
But the ladder can't go down forever.

799
00:44:17,311 --> 00:44:21,274
The average energy is h-bar omega times the length squared of a psi, plus

800
00:44:21,274 --> 00:44:24,149
a half, and a length squared is never negative.

801
00:44:24,149 --> 00:44:27,299
So there's a bottom rung, where a has nothing left to lower:

802
00:44:27,299 --> 00:44:29,449
a psi zero equals zero.

803
00:44:29,449 --> 00:44:33,086
Written out, that's a first-order differential equation, and its solution

804
00:44:33,086 --> 00:44:36,649
is a Gaussian, with energy one half h-bar omega.

805
00:44:36,649 --> 00:44:41,349
Every other state is a dagger applied n times, with energy h-bar omega times

806
00:44:41,349 --> 00:44:43,136
n plus one half.

807
00:44:43,136 --> 00:44:47,636
We never solved a second-order differential equation.

808
00:44:48,070 --> 00:44:49,295
Here's the result:

809
00:44:49,295 --> 00:44:53,757
a parabola with evenly spaced rungs, and the states that go with them.

810
00:44:53,757 --> 00:44:58,207
Each application of a dagger adds one more node, one more sign change:

811
00:44:58,207 --> 00:45:00,357
these are the Hermite functions.

812
00:45:00,357 --> 00:45:05,120
Evenly spaced energies are why a vibrating molecule absorbs light at one sharp

813
00:45:05,120 --> 00:45:07,470
frequency.

814
00:45:07,878 --> 00:45:12,378
As a check, build the Hamiltonian as a big matrix on a fine grid, the same

815
00:45:12,378 --> 00:45:16,553
way we did for the box, and ask a computer for its eigenvalues.

816
00:45:16,553 --> 00:45:22,478
One half, three halves, five halves, to four decimal places.

817
00:45:23,061 --> 00:45:25,049
Look closely at the ground state.

818
00:45:25,049 --> 00:45:28,786
A classical particle with this energy would turn around at these two points,

819
00:45:28,786 --> 00:45:31,061
where all its energy is potential.

820
00:45:31,061 --> 00:45:33,424
The quantum wavefunction doesn't stop there:

821
00:45:33,424 --> 00:45:36,836
its Gaussian tails reach into the region where the kinetic energy would be

822
00:45:36,836 --> 00:45:37,886
negative.

823
00:45:37,886 --> 00:45:41,686
The probability of finding the particle out there is the complementary error

824
00:45:41,686 --> 00:45:42,924
function of one:

825
00:45:42,924 --> 00:45:45,024
15.7 percent.

826
00:45:45,024 --> 00:45:46,674
Tunneling isn't exotic;

827
00:45:46,674 --> 00:45:50,311
it's built into every bound state.

828
00:45:50,845 --> 00:45:54,432
And high up the ladder, quantum mechanics starts to look classical.

829
00:45:54,432 --> 00:45:56,282
Here's the thirtieth state.

830
00:45:56,282 --> 00:46:00,245
A classical oscillator with the same energy spends most of its time near the

831
00:46:00,245 --> 00:46:04,407
turning points, where it's slowest, so its probability density piles up at

832
00:46:04,407 --> 00:46:05,582
the edges.

833
00:46:05,582 --> 00:46:09,970
The quantum density oscillates rapidly, but on average it follows the classical

834
00:46:09,970 --> 00:46:10,920
curve.

835
00:46:10,920 --> 00:46:14,520
This is Bohr's correspondence principle, made visible.

836
00:46:16,567 --> 00:46:19,342
How does the classical world emerge from all this?

837
00:46:19,342 --> 00:46:23,029
Start with the equation for averages from the commutator chapter.

838
00:46:23,029 --> 00:46:27,292
For position, the commutator of H with x gives the average momentum over the

839
00:46:27,292 --> 00:46:27,992
mass:

840
00:46:27,992 --> 00:46:29,179
velocity.

841
00:46:29,179 --> 00:46:33,154
For momentum, the commutator with the potential gives minus the average slope

842
00:46:33,154 --> 00:46:34,167
of the potential:

843
00:46:34,167 --> 00:46:35,267
the force.

844
00:46:35,267 --> 00:46:37,354
This is Ehrenfest's theorem:

845
00:46:37,354 --> 00:46:39,892
averages obey Newton's laws.

846
00:46:39,892 --> 00:46:41,217
With one catch:

847
00:46:41,217 --> 00:46:45,304
the average of the force isn't the force at the average position, unless the

848
00:46:45,304 --> 00:46:46,767
force is linear in x.

849
00:46:46,767 --> 00:46:51,267
For the harmonic oscillator it is, so the averages follow the classical motion

850
00:46:51,267 --> 00:46:53,692
exactly.

851
00:46:54,233 --> 00:46:57,858
Here's a Gaussian packet in a harmonic potential, displaced from the center

852
00:46:57,858 --> 00:47:02,183
and released, simulated by solving the Schrödinger equation numerically.

853
00:47:02,183 --> 00:47:05,983
It swings back and forth exactly in step with a classical particle, and its

854
00:47:05,983 --> 00:47:07,458
shape never changes:

855
00:47:07,458 --> 00:47:09,933
the width stays at its minimum, forever.

856
00:47:09,933 --> 00:47:11,871
These are called coherent states:

857
00:47:11,871 --> 00:47:16,083
eigenvectors of the lowering operator, whose label alpha simply rotates in

858
00:47:16,083 --> 00:47:17,021
time.

859
00:47:17,021 --> 00:47:20,821
They're the most classical states quantum mechanics allows, and they describe

860
00:47:20,821 --> 00:47:22,800
the light from a laser.

861
00:47:22,800 --> 00:47:26,700
Start with a narrower packet instead, and the center still follows the classical

862
00:47:26,700 --> 00:47:30,950
particle exactly, as Ehrenfest promised, but the width breathes:

863
00:47:30,950 --> 00:47:34,150
wide, narrow, wide, twice per swing.

864
00:47:34,150 --> 00:47:35,650
Averages are classical;

865
00:47:35,650 --> 00:47:38,375
the shape is not.

866
00:47:40,567 --> 00:47:43,317
There's a beautiful way to see all of this at once:

867
00:47:43,317 --> 00:47:47,979
put position and momentum on the same picture, like a classical phase space.

868
00:47:47,979 --> 00:47:51,992
The Wigner function, defined by this integral over the wavefunction, lives

869
00:47:51,992 --> 00:47:52,817
there.

870
00:47:52,817 --> 00:47:56,542
Its shadows are exactly the two probability distributions:

871
00:47:56,542 --> 00:48:00,342
squash it onto the x axis and you get the position density;

872
00:48:00,342 --> 00:48:03,833
onto the p axis, the momentum density.

873
00:48:03,833 --> 00:48:08,283
For our coherent state, it's a round blob, and it simply rotates around the

874
00:48:08,283 --> 00:48:13,371
origin, riding on the circle a classical particle traces in phase space.

875
00:48:13,371 --> 00:48:15,421
A classical state is a point;

876
00:48:15,421 --> 00:48:20,208
a quantum state is smeared over an area about the size of h-bar, the uncertainty

877
00:48:20,208 --> 00:48:23,408
principle in picture form.

878
00:48:23,542 --> 00:48:26,954
But the Wigner function is not a probability distribution.

879
00:48:26,954 --> 00:48:29,829
Here's the first excited state of the oscillator:

880
00:48:29,829 --> 00:48:33,967
a ring, and right in the middle it goes negative, down to minus one over pi

881
00:48:33,967 --> 00:48:35,092
h-bar.

882
00:48:35,092 --> 00:48:37,542
No classical probability can do that.

883
00:48:37,542 --> 00:48:42,479
And here's a superposition of two displaced packets, a Schrödinger cat state.

884
00:48:42,479 --> 00:48:45,729
Between the two blobs are stripes of alternating sign:

885
00:48:45,729 --> 00:48:48,492
that's the interference between the two parts.

886
00:48:48,492 --> 00:48:53,342
The sign flips as you move along p, so the interference shows up in the momentum

887
00:48:53,342 --> 00:48:58,217
distribution, while the position distribution shows just two lumps.

888
00:48:58,217 --> 00:49:03,029
Negative regions and fringes are the signatures of genuinely quantum states.

889
00:49:03,029 --> 00:49:06,879
In the classical limit, when the distances involved are huge compared with

890
00:49:06,879 --> 00:49:12,142
the scale set by h-bar, the blobs shrink toward points and the fringes become

891
00:49:12,142 --> 00:49:16,617
far too fine for any measurement to resolve, and the familiar classical world

892
00:49:16,617 --> 00:49:17,467
appears.

893
00:49:19,748 --> 00:49:21,323
Now the system that started it all:

894
00:49:21,323 --> 00:49:22,785
the hydrogen atom.

895
00:49:22,785 --> 00:49:26,135
One electron, held by a proton through the Coulomb force.

896
00:49:26,135 --> 00:49:29,448
In three dimensions, the energy equation reads like this.

897
00:49:29,448 --> 00:49:32,948
Before solving anything, ask what length the answer can depend on.

898
00:49:32,948 --> 00:49:36,898
From h-bar, the electron's mass and the strength of the electric force, there's

899
00:49:36,898 --> 00:49:38,673
only one way to make a length:

900
00:49:38,673 --> 00:49:41,248
the Bohr radius, about half an angstrom.

901
00:49:41,248 --> 00:49:46,498
And the natural energy is the Coulomb energy at that distance, 27.2 electron

902
00:49:46,498 --> 00:49:48,673
volts.

903
00:49:49,073 --> 00:49:52,910
The potential depends only on the distance r, so separate the wavefunction

904
00:49:52,910 --> 00:49:55,598
into a radial part and an angular part.

905
00:49:55,598 --> 00:50:00,035
The angular parts are the spherical harmonics, labeled by two numbers, l and

906
00:50:00,035 --> 00:50:04,698
m, for the total angular momentum and its component along an axis.

907
00:50:04,698 --> 00:50:07,260
Notice the factor e to the i m phi:

908
00:50:07,260 --> 00:50:11,435
as you go around the vertical axis, the phase winds m times.

909
00:50:11,435 --> 00:50:16,498
Angular momentum is written in the phase, just as linear momentum was.

910
00:50:16,498 --> 00:50:21,423
What's left is a one-dimensional problem for u, r times R, in an effective

911
00:50:21,423 --> 00:50:22,660
potential:

912
00:50:22,660 --> 00:50:27,335
the Coulomb well plus a centrifugal barrier that keeps electrons with angular

913
00:50:27,335 --> 00:50:30,873
momentum away from the nucleus.

914
00:50:31,331 --> 00:50:33,618
Here's a short route to the ground state.

915
00:50:33,618 --> 00:50:37,206
Guess a solution that behaves like a power of r near the nucleus and decays

916
00:50:37,206 --> 00:50:39,168
exponentially far away.

917
00:50:39,168 --> 00:50:40,731
Differentiate it twice.

918
00:50:40,731 --> 00:50:42,481
Put it into the radial equation:

919
00:50:42,481 --> 00:50:45,481
you get a constant, plus a term proportional to one over r.

920
00:50:45,481 --> 00:50:49,868
For the equation to hold at every r, that term's coefficient must vanish.

921
00:50:49,868 --> 00:50:54,568
That fixes the decay rate, one over l plus one Bohr radii, and the energy:

922
00:50:54,568 --> 00:50:59,431
minus 13.6 electron volts over l plus one, squared.

923
00:50:59,431 --> 00:51:02,293
For l equal to zero, that's the ground state:

924
00:51:02,293 --> 00:51:07,506
a simple exponential, with energy minus 13.6 electron volts.

925
00:51:07,506 --> 00:51:11,481
The probability of finding the electron at distance r, counting the whole

926
00:51:11,481 --> 00:51:15,606
shell of that radius, peaks at exactly one Bohr radius:

927
00:51:15,606 --> 00:51:22,381
the radius of Bohr's 1913 orbit, now as the most likely distance in a cloud.

928
00:51:22,964 --> 00:51:26,714
For the other states, the same idea works with a polynomial in front of the

929
00:51:26,714 --> 00:51:27,977
exponential.

930
00:51:27,977 --> 00:51:31,227
Integrate outward from the nucleus at a trial energy:

931
00:51:31,227 --> 00:51:35,539
if the energy is a little off, the solution blows up far from the atom, one

932
00:51:35,539 --> 00:51:37,102
way or the other.

933
00:51:37,102 --> 00:51:39,802
Only at special energies does it decay.

934
00:51:39,802 --> 00:51:43,102
Requiring that the series stops gives the famous result:

935
00:51:43,102 --> 00:51:47,298
minus 13.6 electron volts over n squared.

936
00:51:47,298 --> 00:51:49,323
And here's something remarkable.

937
00:51:49,323 --> 00:51:54,060
Solve the radial equation numerically for l equals zero, one and two, and

938
00:51:54,060 --> 00:51:55,635
the energies line up:

939
00:51:55,635 --> 00:51:58,048
they depend only on n, not on l.

940
00:51:58,048 --> 00:52:02,260
That's an accidental degeneracy, the fingerprint of a hidden symmetry of the

941
00:52:02,260 --> 00:52:10,073
one-over-r force that Pauli used in 1926 to solve hydrogen with matrix mechanics.

942
00:52:10,531 --> 00:52:14,518
Here are the stationary states themselves, computed from the exact solutions

943
00:52:14,518 --> 00:52:16,606
and rendered as glowing clouds:

944
00:52:16,606 --> 00:52:20,731
the brightness is the probability density, the color is the phase.

945
00:52:20,731 --> 00:52:24,893
The first number is the energy level, the second the angular momentum, the

946
00:52:24,893 --> 00:52:28,831
third how many times the phase winds around the vertical axis.

947
00:52:28,831 --> 00:52:31,881
For each energy level there are n squared of them.

948
00:52:31,881 --> 00:52:33,131
These aren't orbits;

949
00:52:33,131 --> 00:52:39,331
they're stationary waves, and each would only rotate its colors as time passes.

950
00:52:39,839 --> 00:52:43,527
And where do chemistry's dumbbell-shaped p orbitals come from?

951
00:52:43,527 --> 00:52:46,977
Take the states with m equals minus one and plus one:

952
00:52:46,977 --> 00:52:51,152
two rings of probability whose phases wind in opposite directions.

953
00:52:51,152 --> 00:52:52,364
Subtract them.

954
00:52:52,364 --> 00:52:55,239
On one side the phases agree and the waves add;

955
00:52:55,239 --> 00:52:57,027
on the other they cancel.

956
00:52:57,027 --> 00:53:01,652
The result is the dumbbell, a superposition of two angular momenta.

957
00:53:01,652 --> 00:53:04,439
Interference again.

958
00:53:05,023 --> 00:53:05,998
Finally, light.

959
00:53:05,998 --> 00:53:07,310
Remember the box:

960
00:53:07,310 --> 00:53:11,935
a superposition of two energies sloshes back and forth at the difference frequency.

961
00:53:11,935 --> 00:53:13,948
Here's the same thing in hydrogen:

962
00:53:13,948 --> 00:53:16,548
the ground state plus the 2 p state.

963
00:53:16,548 --> 00:53:21,173
The electron cloud swings up and down, a tiny oscillating charge, at the Bohr

964
00:53:21,173 --> 00:53:24,260
frequency, E two minus E one over h:

965
00:53:24,260 --> 00:53:27,898
about two and a half million billion times per second.

966
00:53:27,898 --> 00:53:31,960
An oscillating charge radiates, at exactly that frequency:

967
00:53:31,960 --> 00:53:39,031
121.6 nanometers, the Lyman alpha line, the strongest line hydrogen emits.

968
00:53:39,031 --> 00:53:41,868
Every spectral line is a Bohr frequency.

969
00:53:41,868 --> 00:53:46,006
Transitions down to the second level give the visible Balmer lines:

970
00:53:46,006 --> 00:53:55,056
the red line at 656 nanometers, then 486, 434 and 410, exactly where astronomers

971
00:53:55,056 --> 00:53:58,743
find them in glowing hydrogen clouds across the sky.

972
00:53:58,743 --> 00:54:02,356
The colors of the universe are differences between eigenvalues.

973
00:54:07,561 --> 00:54:12,248
In February 1922, in Frankfurt, Otto Stern and Walther Gerlach sent a beam

974
00:54:12,248 --> 00:54:16,148
of silver atoms through a deliberately uneven magnetic field.

975
00:54:16,148 --> 00:54:20,548
Each atom is a tiny magnet, and the uneven field pushes it up or down depending

976
00:54:20,548 --> 00:54:22,573
on which way its magnet points.

977
00:54:22,573 --> 00:54:26,823
Classically, the magnets point every which way, so you'd expect a continuous

978
00:54:26,823 --> 00:54:28,311
smear on the screen.

979
00:54:28,311 --> 00:54:30,773
What they saw was two separate spots.

980
00:54:30,773 --> 00:54:35,736
Every atom was deflected either fully up or fully down, never in between.

981
00:54:35,736 --> 00:54:38,286
The explanation came a few years later:

982
00:54:38,286 --> 00:54:43,486
the electron carries an intrinsic angular momentum, its spin, whose component

983
00:54:43,486 --> 00:54:48,936
along any axis you measure is only ever plus or minus h-bar over two.

984
00:54:48,936 --> 00:54:50,661
It's not a little spinning ball;

985
00:54:50,661 --> 00:54:54,698
it's a quantum system with exactly two outcomes, and the simplest one there

986
00:54:54,698 --> 00:54:56,786
is.

987
00:54:56,927 --> 00:55:00,690
A spin state is a vector in a two-dimensional complex space:

988
00:55:00,690 --> 00:55:05,165
alpha times up plus beta times down, with the squared lengths adding to one.

989
00:55:05,165 --> 00:55:07,827
Two complex numbers is four real numbers;

990
00:55:07,827 --> 00:55:12,077
the length condition removes one, and the overall phase, which doesn't matter,

991
00:55:12,077 --> 00:55:13,552
removes another.

992
00:55:13,552 --> 00:55:17,852
Two angles remain, and every state can be written as cosine theta over two

993
00:55:17,852 --> 00:55:22,886
times up, plus e to the i phi sine theta over two times down.

994
00:55:22,886 --> 00:55:24,986
Two angles are a point on a sphere:

995
00:55:24,986 --> 00:55:26,248
the Bloch sphere.

996
00:55:26,248 --> 00:55:30,148
Up is the north pole, down the south pole, and their equal superpositions

997
00:55:30,148 --> 00:55:31,836
lie around the equator.

998
00:55:31,836 --> 00:55:33,498
Notice the half angles.

999
00:55:33,498 --> 00:55:37,548
Up and down are perpendicular vectors in the state space, but they sit at

1000
00:55:37,548 --> 00:55:38,911
opposite poles:

1001
00:55:38,911 --> 00:55:42,461
the sphere doubles every angle.

1002
00:55:42,569 --> 00:55:45,332
The spin observables are two-by-two matrices:

1003
00:55:45,332 --> 00:55:48,144
h-bar over two times the Pauli matrices.

1004
00:55:48,144 --> 00:55:50,869
The eigenvectors of sigma z are up and down;

1005
00:55:50,869 --> 00:55:54,519
the eigenvectors of sigma x are their sum and difference, on the sphere's

1006
00:55:54,519 --> 00:55:55,444
equator.

1007
00:55:55,444 --> 00:55:57,182
And the components don't commute:

1008
00:55:57,182 --> 00:56:00,557
S x S y minus S y S x is i h-bar S z.

1009
00:56:00,557 --> 00:56:05,357
So, by the uncertainty relation we derived, no state has definite values of

1010
00:56:05,357 --> 00:56:07,094
two different components.

1011
00:56:07,094 --> 00:56:11,919
Knowing spin up along z means knowing nothing about x.

1012
00:56:12,402 --> 00:56:13,952
Now chain measurements.

1013
00:56:13,952 --> 00:56:18,302
Prepare atoms with spin up along z, then measure along a direction n tilted

1014
00:56:18,302 --> 00:56:19,990
by an angle theta.

1015
00:56:19,990 --> 00:56:24,240
The Born rule says the probability of up along n is the squared overlap, and

1016
00:56:24,240 --> 00:56:27,890
with the half angles from the sphere, that's cosine squared of theta over

1017
00:56:27,890 --> 00:56:28,952
two.

1018
00:56:28,952 --> 00:56:33,165
A thousand simulated atoms at each angle land right on the curve:

1019
00:56:33,165 --> 00:56:38,394
always up at zero degrees, fifty-fifty at ninety, never at one eighty.

1020
00:56:38,394 --> 00:56:40,169
Here's the strangest version.

1021
00:56:40,169 --> 00:56:42,082
Select spin up along z.

1022
00:56:42,082 --> 00:56:46,469
Then measure along x, and keep only the atoms that came out up along x.

1023
00:56:46,469 --> 00:56:47,994
Now measure z again.

1024
00:56:47,994 --> 00:56:51,144
Before, every one of these atoms was up along z.

1025
00:56:51,144 --> 00:56:52,632
Now it's fifty-fifty:

1026
00:56:52,632 --> 00:56:55,919
487 up, 513 down.

1027
00:56:55,919 --> 00:56:58,307
The x measurement didn't just reveal something;

1028
00:56:58,307 --> 00:57:03,069
it replaced the state, and the z information is gone.

1029
00:57:03,552 --> 00:57:07,065
Finally, put the spin in a magnetic field along z.

1030
00:57:07,065 --> 00:57:11,452
The Hamiltonian is proportional to S z, so the up and down components just

1031
00:57:11,452 --> 00:57:15,790
pick up opposite phases, and on the Bloch sphere the arrow precesses around

1032
00:57:15,790 --> 00:57:17,690
the field like a spinning top:

1033
00:57:17,690 --> 00:57:23,452
Larmor precession, about 28 billion turns per second per tesla for an electron.

1034
00:57:23,452 --> 00:57:27,869
It's how MRI machines read the spins of protons.

1035
00:57:27,869 --> 00:57:30,094
But watch the two components themselves.

1036
00:57:30,094 --> 00:57:32,207
They turn at half the rate of the arrow.

1037
00:57:32,207 --> 00:57:36,532
After one full turn of the arrow, three hundred sixty degrees, both components

1038
00:57:36,532 --> 00:57:38,269
have turned only halfway:

1039
00:57:38,269 --> 00:57:40,794
the state has come back to minus itself.

1040
00:57:40,794 --> 00:57:44,582
It takes seven hundred twenty degrees to get back to where you started.

1041
00:57:44,582 --> 00:57:48,907
An overall minus sign is invisible on its own, but not in interference with

1042
00:57:48,907 --> 00:57:50,507
an unrotated beam:

1043
00:57:50,507 --> 00:57:54,519
neutron interferometers confirmed the sign flip in 1975.

1044
00:57:56,166 --> 00:57:59,704
With two particles, quantum mechanics gets truly strange.

1045
00:57:59,704 --> 00:58:01,104
The key point first:

1046
00:58:01,104 --> 00:58:03,779
two particles don't have two wavefunctions.

1047
00:58:03,779 --> 00:58:08,779
They share one, a single complex number for every pair of positions, x one

1048
00:58:08,779 --> 00:58:10,066
and x two.

1049
00:58:10,066 --> 00:58:14,666
If it factors into a function of x one times a function of x two, the particles

1050
00:58:14,666 --> 00:58:15,966
are independent.

1051
00:58:15,966 --> 00:58:19,066
But most two-particle states don't factor.

1052
00:58:19,066 --> 00:58:22,304
In this one, the probability lies along the diagonal:

1053
00:58:22,304 --> 00:58:28,291
find particle one at 1.5, and particle two is pinned near 1.5 too, though

1054
00:58:28,291 --> 00:58:30,916
on its own it could have been almost anywhere.

1055
00:58:30,916 --> 00:58:33,816
That's entanglement.

1056
00:58:34,233 --> 00:58:36,445
The cleanest example uses spin.

1057
00:58:36,445 --> 00:58:40,008
Two spin-half particles can be prepared in the singlet state, with total spin

1058
00:58:40,008 --> 00:58:40,658
zero:

1059
00:58:40,658 --> 00:58:42,308
up-down minus down-up.

1060
00:58:42,308 --> 00:58:46,445
They fly apart, to Alice and to Bob, who each measure spin along an axis of

1061
00:58:46,445 --> 00:58:47,595
their choosing.

1062
00:58:47,595 --> 00:58:48,995
Work out the Born rule:

1063
00:58:48,995 --> 00:58:53,220
if their axes differ by an angle theta, they get the same answer with probability

1064
00:58:53,220 --> 00:58:57,933
sine squared of theta over two, and opposite answers with cosine squared.

1065
00:58:57,933 --> 00:59:01,683
Score plus one for same and minus one for opposite.

1066
00:59:01,683 --> 00:59:05,545
The average score, the correlation, is minus cosine theta.

1067
00:59:05,545 --> 00:59:10,333
Yet each side, looked at alone, is a perfect coin flip.

1068
00:59:10,791 --> 00:59:15,779
In 1935 Einstein, Podolsky and Rosen argued that such correlations mean quantum

1069
00:59:15,779 --> 00:59:17,616
mechanics is incomplete:

1070
00:59:17,616 --> 00:59:21,404
surely each pair leaves the source carrying hidden instructions, lambda, that

1071
00:59:21,404 --> 00:59:24,104
decide every possible answer in advance.

1072
00:59:24,104 --> 00:59:28,329
In 1964 John Bell showed that this idea can be tested.

1073
00:59:28,329 --> 00:59:32,466
Suppose Alice's answer depends only on her own setting and the instructions,

1074
00:59:32,466 --> 00:59:33,954
and Bob's on his.

1075
00:59:33,954 --> 00:59:39,724
Give Alice two possible settings, a and a prime, and Bob two, b and b prime.

1076
00:59:39,724 --> 00:59:43,399
For any one pair, all four answers are plus or minus one.

1077
00:59:43,399 --> 00:59:44,862
Look at this combination:

1078
00:59:44,862 --> 00:59:48,987
B and B prime either agree or disagree, so one of the brackets is zero and

1079
00:59:48,987 --> 00:59:51,174
the other is plus or minus two.

1080
00:59:51,174 --> 00:59:53,937
The whole thing is always plus or minus two.

1081
00:59:53,937 --> 00:59:57,824
Average over many pairs, and the combination of four correlations, called

1082
00:59:57,824 --> 01:00:00,712
S, can never exceed two in size.

1083
01:00:00,712 --> 01:00:05,624
That's the CHSH inequality, and it follows from nothing but pre-existing answers

1084
01:00:05,624 --> 01:00:08,149
and locality.

1085
01:00:08,633 --> 01:00:11,158
Now ask what quantum mechanics predicts.

1086
01:00:11,158 --> 01:00:14,083
Choose the four directions forty-five degrees apart.

1087
01:00:14,083 --> 01:00:19,358
Three of the correlations are minus cosine forty-five, about minus 0.707,

1088
01:00:19,358 --> 01:00:24,445
and the fourth, at one hundred thirty-five degrees, is plus 0.707, which enters

1089
01:00:24,445 --> 01:00:26,195
S with a minus sign.

1090
01:00:26,195 --> 01:00:28,570
All four contributions point the same way:

1091
01:00:28,570 --> 01:00:31,595
S is two root two, about 2.83.

1092
01:00:31,595 --> 01:00:32,820
More than two.

1093
01:00:32,820 --> 01:00:37,308
No theory of pre-existing local answers can produce these correlations.

1094
01:00:37,308 --> 01:00:42,808
And two root two is itself a hard ceiling for quantum mechanics.

1095
01:00:43,233 --> 01:00:45,808
We can watch the difference in a simulation.

1096
01:00:45,808 --> 01:00:50,158
Sample a hundred thousand pairs per setting from the Born rule, and the correlation

1097
01:00:50,158 --> 01:00:52,708
traces out minus cosine theta.

1098
01:00:52,708 --> 01:00:55,545
Now a classical model with hidden instructions:

1099
01:00:55,545 --> 01:01:00,020
each pair carries a random direction, and each side answers according to which

1100
01:01:00,020 --> 01:01:02,258
side of it their axis falls on.

1101
01:01:02,258 --> 01:01:05,058
Its correlation is a straight line.

1102
01:01:05,058 --> 01:01:09,508
The two agree at zero, ninety and one hundred eighty degrees, but the cosine

1103
01:01:09,508 --> 01:01:14,658
bulges past the straight line in between, and that bulge is exactly what CHSH

1104
01:01:14,658 --> 01:01:15,708
detects.

1105
01:01:15,708 --> 01:01:16,945
From the samples:

1106
01:01:16,945 --> 01:01:23,283
S is 2.828 for quantum mechanics, and 1.996 for hidden instructions, within

1107
01:01:23,283 --> 01:01:25,491
noise of the bound of two.

1108
01:01:25,491 --> 01:01:30,591
And in both cases, Alice's results on their own are always fifty-fifty, whatever

1109
01:01:30,591 --> 01:01:31,779
Bob does.

1110
01:01:31,779 --> 01:01:36,129
The correlation only appears when they compare notes, so entanglement can't

1111
01:01:36,129 --> 01:01:39,166
be used to send a message.

1112
01:01:39,624 --> 01:01:41,324
Experiment decided it.

1113
01:01:41,324 --> 01:01:45,724
Freedman and Clauser saw the violation with photons in 1972.

1114
01:01:45,724 --> 01:01:49,024
Alain Aspect's group switched the settings while the photons were in flight

1115
01:01:49,024 --> 01:01:50,687
in 1982.

1116
01:01:50,687 --> 01:01:55,262
In 2015, a team in Delft closed the remaining loopholes with electron spins

1117
01:01:55,262 --> 01:02:02,649
1.3 kilometers apart, measuring S equals 2.42, plus or minus 0.20, and groups

1118
01:02:02,649 --> 01:02:06,499
in Vienna and at NIST did the same with photons.

1119
01:02:06,499 --> 01:02:11,424
The 2022 Nobel Prize went to Aspect, Clauser and Zeilinger.

1120
01:02:11,424 --> 01:02:13,524
Nature violates Bell's bound:

1121
01:02:13,524 --> 01:02:17,424
the outcomes are not simply written in advance, in any local way.

1122
01:02:19,580 --> 01:02:22,055
Let's step back and see how far we've come.

1123
01:02:22,055 --> 01:02:23,980
The whole theory fits on one line:

1124
01:02:23,980 --> 01:02:28,155
the Schrödinger equation, the Born rule, and the commutation relation between

1125
01:02:28,155 --> 01:02:29,980
position and momentum.

1126
01:02:29,980 --> 01:02:33,493
States are vectors whose amplitudes add before you square them.

1127
01:02:33,493 --> 01:02:35,943
Observables are Hermitian operators.

1128
01:02:35,943 --> 01:02:39,693
Time evolution turns each energy component at its own rate.

1129
01:02:39,693 --> 01:02:42,568
Position and momentum can't both be sharp.

1130
01:02:42,568 --> 01:02:45,980
Energies are quantized when a wave has to fit.

1131
01:02:45,980 --> 01:02:50,530
And entangled particles share a single wavefunction, with correlations no

1132
01:02:50,530 --> 01:02:53,505
local model can match.

1133
01:02:53,964 --> 01:02:58,001
And every picture in this video, the wave through the slits, the carpet, the

1134
01:02:58,001 --> 01:03:02,639
orbitals, the phase-space pictures, came from solving those equations, not

1135
01:03:02,639 --> 01:03:04,664
from an artist's impression.

1136
01:03:04,664 --> 01:03:08,839
A hundred years after Schrödinger, the same equation runs the transistors

1137
01:03:08,839 --> 01:03:15,914
in your phone, the lasers in fiber-optic cables and the magnets in MRI scanners.

1138
01:03:16,330 --> 01:03:17,480
There's much more.

1139
01:03:17,480 --> 01:03:21,230
Identical particles and the Pauli exclusion principle, which make chemistry

1140
01:03:21,230 --> 01:03:22,180
possible.

1141
01:03:22,180 --> 01:03:24,443
Perturbation theory, for real atoms.

1142
01:03:24,443 --> 01:03:28,343
Dirac's relativistic equation, and quantum field theory beyond it.

1143
01:03:28,343 --> 01:03:31,980
Decoherence, and the still-open question of what measurement really is.

1144
01:03:31,980 --> 01:03:35,780
And quantum computing, which is the same linear algebra you've just seen,

1145
01:03:35,780 --> 01:03:37,655
in two to the n dimensions.

1146
01:03:37,655 --> 01:03:39,993
But all of it is built on what's on this screen:

1147
01:03:39,993 --> 01:03:44,130
complex amplitudes, evolving by the Schrödinger equation.

1148
01:03:44,130 --> 01:03:45,305
Thanks for watching.
